WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.410 --> 00:00:02.410
PICKERING: Good afternoon.

00:00:02.410 --> 00:00:04.450
I hope you've had a lovely lunch.

00:00:04.450 --> 00:00:06.710
We're about to make it pay.

00:00:06.710 --> 00:00:08.120
(Laugher.)

00:00:08.120 --> 00:00:10.330
The panel today is on Turkey.

00:00:10.330 --> 00:00:13.860
And I have a few opening words.

00:00:13.860 --> 00:00:21.070
You've seen the names of our panelists-Jim
Jeffrey, Melissa Dalton, and Steven Cook.

00:00:21.070 --> 00:00:25.940
The introductions are in your papers, and
so I won't bother and take up your time to

00:00:25.940 --> 00:00:28.070
move that further.

00:00:28.070 --> 00:00:34.600
On behalf of CFR, I want to thank Richard
Plepler and HBO for their generous support

00:00:34.600 --> 00:00:36.370
of this series.

00:00:36.370 --> 00:00:42.300
I want to welcome CFR members around the nation
and the world participating in this meeting

00:00:42.300 --> 00:00:44.899
through livestream.

00:00:44.899 --> 00:00:52.650
This is meant to simulate, but not reconstruct,
a National Security Council meeting on Turkey.

00:00:52.650 --> 00:00:56.220
Any resemblance to the reality will be purely
hypothetical.

00:00:56.220 --> 00:01:03.829
But I will start by sketching a few thoughts
on Turkey today and its challenges, and a

00:01:03.829 --> 00:01:07.520
few thoughts about U.S. current policy to
Turkey.

00:01:07.520 --> 00:01:13.650
I will ask each of the panelists to respond,
both to correct my errors and omissions in

00:01:13.650 --> 00:01:20.230
the analysis, but also give us their thoughts
on the policy and what might be done to correct

00:01:20.230 --> 00:01:21.230
it.

00:01:21.230 --> 00:01:22.540
And we'll try to take that in two jumps.

00:01:22.540 --> 00:01:30.120
I hope sometime between 1:00 and 1:15 to open
the floor to you for your participation, all

00:01:30.120 --> 00:01:37.210
of course as secretaries of state in the arrangement
that we have organized here.

00:01:37.210 --> 00:01:41.330
It's a pleasure to see you all, and thank
you very much for joining us.

00:01:41.330 --> 00:01:47.450
And we will hope to end promptly at 1:45,
as is the Council's usual practice.

00:01:47.450 --> 00:01:48.720
My name is Tom Pickering.

00:01:48.720 --> 00:01:51.810
I'm the national security advisor for this
event.

00:01:51.810 --> 00:01:55.750
The others here can play whatever role they
like, except the president.

00:01:55.750 --> 00:01:58.060
He's off visiting Florida.

00:01:58.060 --> 00:01:59.080
(Laughter.)

00:01:59.080 --> 00:02:05.320
Turkey is a major preoccupation of the United
States, and as well it should be.

00:02:05.320 --> 00:02:10.560
Years ago I was struck by the fact that after
the Second World War, a Hungarian foreign

00:02:10.560 --> 00:02:16.640
minister declared that Hungary was the only
country in the world surrounded by Hungarians.

00:02:16.640 --> 00:02:21.590
The relationship to Turkey is that Turkey
is perhaps the only country in the world completely

00:02:21.590 --> 00:02:23.980
surrounded by problems.

00:02:23.980 --> 00:02:30.300
At one time and another one had seen in every
azimuth serious difficulties.

00:02:30.300 --> 00:02:36.840
Turkey today is in many sense of the word
a major preoccupation of the United States

00:02:36.840 --> 00:02:43.240
in large measure because it is close to the
significant problem sets in the world.

00:02:43.240 --> 00:02:49.600
Three in particular I think are of transcendental
importance to us, and very great significance

00:02:49.600 --> 00:02:56.330
to Turkey: Iraq and ISIS, Syria and ISIS,
and the Kurds.

00:02:56.330 --> 00:03:01.030
And their longstanding preoccupations with
each of these, and their difficulties and

00:03:01.030 --> 00:03:06.270
their changing azimuths in dealing with each
of them are important.

00:03:06.270 --> 00:03:13.760
Turkey in that regard has had a building enmity
over the last four or five years with President

00:03:13.760 --> 00:03:17.150
Assad and sought to unseat him.

00:03:17.150 --> 00:03:24.960
Turkey, with its religiously informed and
tilted government, has from time to time seen

00:03:24.960 --> 00:03:34.180
perhaps ISIS, al-Qaida, Jabhat al-Nusra, Ajnad
al-Sham as less than consequentially as we

00:03:34.180 --> 00:03:41.850
have seen, as organizations devoted to pursuing
Islamic fundamentalist terrorism on their

00:03:41.850 --> 00:03:42.850
borders.

00:03:42.850 --> 00:03:47.210
They've been more preoccupied, perhaps, with
the Kurds and the Kurdish problem, a longstanding

00:03:47.210 --> 00:03:53.300
issue for Turkey which I won't sketch out
because it would take us a couple of NSC meetings

00:03:53.300 --> 00:03:55.480
just to go through the facts in the case.

00:03:55.480 --> 00:04:01.780
But it is very clear that Erdogan and Turkey
are in many, many ways, perhaps, as bothered

00:04:01.780 --> 00:04:07.670
and concerned by the Kurds and perhaps PKK
influence as they have been certainly at any

00:04:07.670 --> 00:04:11.810
time perhaps since they put Mr. Ocalan in
jail.

00:04:11.810 --> 00:04:18.430
And Turkey and Iraq have had a longstanding
and somewhat contentious relationship.

00:04:18.430 --> 00:04:24.550
Mutual interdependence to get energy out and
to deal with Iraqi energy as some kind of

00:04:24.550 --> 00:04:31.710
a source for the Turkish southeast, if not
beyond, and a significant set of concerns

00:04:31.710 --> 00:04:38.230
about the near almost quasi-independent Iraqi
Kurdistan, and its interrelationship with

00:04:38.230 --> 00:04:45.150
Turkey, and its large number of Kurds, and
its preoccupation with the PKK.

00:04:45.150 --> 00:04:50.830
It is, I think, also extremely important that
while the United States, far away, has had

00:04:50.830 --> 00:04:56.830
a longstanding and very important set of relationship
with Turkey, both Turkey as a NATO member,

00:04:56.830 --> 00:05:06.080
Turkey as a putative EU member, Turkey as
a kind of rock or bulwark of what is, perhaps,

00:05:06.080 --> 00:05:11.280
an unusual extension of Europe into the Middle
East.

00:05:11.280 --> 00:05:17.280
And the role that it plays in the Middle East
as a Sunni Islamic country is of significant

00:05:17.280 --> 00:05:20.410
importance to Europe and its role.

00:05:20.410 --> 00:05:25.210
And Turkey's history with Europe, of course,
has been at best checkered.

00:05:25.210 --> 00:05:31.520
And recent efforts obviously on Turkey's part-I
may be too direct in this-to give payback

00:05:31.520 --> 00:05:38.180
to Europe for their "welcome" in the EU, if
I can use that word in quotes, through export

00:05:38.180 --> 00:05:45.710
of Syrian refugees is in one and another ways
also one of the preoccupying factors.

00:05:45.710 --> 00:05:52.510
Just in this arc of Turkey's border we see
in fact what has moved on from a conflict

00:05:52.510 --> 00:06:00.320
which at one time the old Arab statement the
enemy of my enemy can be my friend, that particular

00:06:00.320 --> 00:06:03.230
way of thinking has now disappeared.

00:06:03.230 --> 00:06:09.330
And in fact, it's a war of all against all
in many circumstances, but a confused and

00:06:09.330 --> 00:06:15.830
chaotic picture in which the United States
has great interests, both in the potential

00:06:15.830 --> 00:06:21.460
over the long term for a future political
settlement in Syria, for dealing with ISIS,

00:06:21.460 --> 00:06:28.340
which has not only set a caliphate based in
Raqqa but in many ways is doing two other

00:06:28.340 --> 00:06:34.610
things strategically which the United States
is, and should be in my view, more heavily

00:06:34.610 --> 00:06:40.660
preoccupied with: border jumping by creating
franchises in various parts of the Islamic

00:06:40.660 --> 00:06:46.820
world from as far east as certainly Afghanistan
and maybe Indonesia, to as far west as Mali

00:06:46.820 --> 00:06:55.210
and Nigeria and secondly, using its capacities,
particularly now in Western Europe, to use

00:06:55.210 --> 00:07:03.110
terror attacks as a way both of pushing its
influence, certainly stimulating its recruitment,

00:07:03.110 --> 00:07:10.020
and seeking to find ways to dislodge and disrupt
the harmony and purpose certainly of Europe

00:07:10.020 --> 00:07:11.530
and the West.

00:07:11.530 --> 00:07:18.030
Two other factors I think are important: Turkey
has had not necessarily simply splendid relationships

00:07:18.030 --> 00:07:22.160
with the United States over this region of
the world and other factors.

00:07:22.160 --> 00:07:27.290
But at the same time, the United States has
worked hard, obviously, to continue to keep

00:07:27.290 --> 00:07:32.250
Turkey within the ambit of NATO and in the
general direction the United States would

00:07:32.250 --> 00:07:33.880
like to see it go.

00:07:33.880 --> 00:07:40.919
But Mr. Erdogan marches currently to his own
rather new set of drummers, and is not completely

00:07:40.919 --> 00:07:46.560
in one way or another either predictable or,
in some words, reliable.

00:07:46.560 --> 00:07:53.190
At the same time, Turkey has found perhaps
a new opportunity, or maybe a new danger,

00:07:53.190 --> 00:07:59.200
in its big neighbor to the north, beginning
with the shoot down, but the buildup, obviously,

00:07:59.200 --> 00:08:05.430
of Turkish concern by Russian presence on
both sides of it, something the Turks have

00:08:05.430 --> 00:08:08.199
never liked on one side of it.

00:08:08.199 --> 00:08:12.790
And that will in many ways, I think, be a
useful thing for us to think about in the

00:08:12.790 --> 00:08:17.090
meeting, as to whether it provides some of
the leverage we lack.

00:08:17.090 --> 00:08:25.440
U.S. foreign policy toward Turkey has been
at best difficult to understand.

00:08:25.440 --> 00:08:32.409
Put it this way, mobile and dynamic, perhaps
best framed around bringing Turkey along with

00:08:32.409 --> 00:08:39.349
our own interests in the region, and doing
what we can to get along with a somewhat irascible

00:08:39.349 --> 00:08:46.060
and somewhat changeable gentleman in charge,
who still has a commanding presence in Turkey.

00:08:46.060 --> 00:08:51.959
Doing what we can to build a relationship,
doing what we can to see that that relationship

00:08:51.959 --> 00:08:57.350
is oriented in the directions in which we
would like to go, and certainly not against

00:08:57.350 --> 00:09:05.639
us, and if at best cast in a do-no-harm mode,
where nothing else seems to be reliable and

00:09:05.639 --> 00:09:06.869
useful.

00:09:06.869 --> 00:09:12.509
If you found it difficult to think through
all the elements of U.S. policy toward Turkey,

00:09:12.509 --> 00:09:17.360
I think it would be-it'd be safe to say that
you join the four of us on the stage in the

00:09:17.360 --> 00:09:20.179
simply more difficult conundrum there.

00:09:20.179 --> 00:09:29.350
So now let me, if I can, turn things over
to my friends and colleagues here on the NSC

00:09:29.350 --> 00:09:31.110
for their visions and views.

00:09:31.110 --> 00:09:34.629
And maybe, Steve, I could begin with you.

00:09:34.629 --> 00:09:42.470
Give us your sense of where that analysis
ought to be tweaked, corrected, reoriented,

00:09:42.470 --> 00:09:46.740
and perhaps a little bit of your own sense
of the policy directions that we take.

00:09:46.740 --> 00:09:49.749
COOK: Well, thanks very much for the question.

00:09:49.749 --> 00:09:51.649
And thank you all for being here.

00:09:51.649 --> 00:09:58.290
I now know who's not advising Donald Trump,
who's giving a speech around the block.

00:09:58.290 --> 00:10:00.839
I couldn't wait to say that today, actually.

00:10:00.839 --> 00:10:01.839
(Laughter.)

00:10:01.839 --> 00:10:06.100
But back to the NSC meeting, we have moved.

00:10:06.100 --> 00:10:10.490
Our relationship with Turkey has moved over
the course of this presidency.

00:10:10.490 --> 00:10:15.339
It has never been an easy relationship, but
I think there was a broader, more expansive

00:10:15.339 --> 00:10:19.920
sense of how we can work with the Turks at
the beginning of the Obama presidency that

00:10:19.920 --> 00:10:22.040
changed significantly toward the end of it.

00:10:22.040 --> 00:10:27.889
And that was, if you remember, the president
went to Ankara in April 2009 and talked about

00:10:27.889 --> 00:10:32.499
common values and common vision, and the way
in which the two countries can work together

00:10:32.499 --> 00:10:34.660
in a variety of areas.

00:10:34.660 --> 00:10:43.050
And at the time, the National Security Council
produced three essential points around which

00:10:43.050 --> 00:10:45.420
Turkey policy would be conducted.

00:10:45.420 --> 00:10:49.480
One is anchoring Turkey firmly in the West.

00:10:49.480 --> 00:10:54.330
Two is enlisting Turkey to help advance American
strategic interests in both Europe and the

00:10:54.330 --> 00:10:55.360
Middle East.

00:10:55.360 --> 00:11:01.230
And third, to hold out Turkey not necessarily
as a model, but as an inspiration to countries

00:11:01.230 --> 00:11:07.129
to its south and east, that is as a prosperous
and democratic predominately Muslim country.

00:11:07.129 --> 00:11:08.930
I think we have moved from that.

00:11:08.930 --> 00:11:15.800
None of those are as clear that we can do
those things, given the changes in Turkey

00:11:15.800 --> 00:11:19.070
in particular, and changes in the region as
well.

00:11:19.070 --> 00:11:24.449
And I think we are now moved to what is essentially
a transactional relationship between Washington

00:11:24.449 --> 00:11:25.449
and Ankara.

00:11:25.449 --> 00:11:28.430
There are certain things that we would like
to do with the Turks.

00:11:28.430 --> 00:11:31.689
There are certain things that we've identified
that are in the common interest.

00:11:31.689 --> 00:11:36.380
And even things that are in the common interest
are often difficult for the United States

00:11:36.380 --> 00:11:40.980
to do, so we've chosen not to rock the boat
with the Turks on things that are less important

00:11:40.980 --> 00:11:41.980
to us.

00:11:41.980 --> 00:11:49.030
So the primary example of that is, of course,
we have been relatively mute about the profound

00:11:49.030 --> 00:11:56.399
changes in Turkey's domestic politics, in
its clear authoritarian turn over the course

00:11:56.399 --> 00:12:00.799
of the last six or seven years because we
have prioritized some of those strategic issues

00:12:00.799 --> 00:12:08.350
that you outlined, mostly having to do with
the conflicts in Syria and Iraq.

00:12:08.350 --> 00:12:09.350
PICKERING: Melissa.

00:12:09.350 --> 00:12:10.660
DALTON: Thank you.

00:12:10.660 --> 00:12:12.559
It's a pleasure to be with you all here today.

00:12:12.559 --> 00:12:17.339
I'm going to talk a bit about the defense
policy perspective with regards to Turkey.

00:12:17.339 --> 00:12:22.379
It's certainly in the interest of the United
States to pursue close strategic ties to Turkey.

00:12:22.379 --> 00:12:24.110
It's one of the top 20 economies in the world.

00:12:24.110 --> 00:12:30.339
It's a key NATO ally that sits at the nexus
of challenges in the Middle East and Europe,

00:12:30.339 --> 00:12:36.779
predominately a Muslim member of NATO that
can bridge some of the hot spots in the Middle

00:12:36.779 --> 00:12:38.399
East, Caucasus, and Eastern Europe.

00:12:38.399 --> 00:12:45.079
U.S.-Turkey relations are often contentious,
for most of the history of the relationship.

00:12:45.079 --> 00:12:48.939
But arguably, currently we're at a bit of
a low point.

00:12:48.939 --> 00:12:55.369
Tensions of the PYD, the United States support
for the Kurdish affiliate in Syria are the

00:12:55.369 --> 00:12:57.860
tip of the iceberg of those tensions.

00:12:57.860 --> 00:13:03.600
The Turks saw the NATO response to the violation
of its airspace, the downing of its aircraft,

00:13:03.600 --> 00:13:04.600
as weak.

00:13:04.600 --> 00:13:08.670
And the Patriot redeployment didn't go over
well.

00:13:08.670 --> 00:13:14.149
Security is really the hub of the relationship,
but it's often hindered by bureaucratic scenes

00:13:14.149 --> 00:13:15.949
in the Department of Defense.

00:13:15.949 --> 00:13:22.420
Too often the U.S. defense approach to Turkey
is complicated by the fact that Turkey sits

00:13:22.420 --> 00:13:29.129
in European command but issue sets often span
into the Central Command space.

00:13:29.129 --> 00:13:34.899
And this may seem to be purely a bureaucratic
issue, but it has strategic effects in terms

00:13:34.899 --> 00:13:41.499
of the overall defense approach to Turkey
and how it affects overall relationships and

00:13:41.499 --> 00:13:46.600
unity and consistency of operational and military
communication with the Turks.

00:13:46.600 --> 00:13:51.350
While the United States and Turkey are working
together to address shared interests, such

00:13:51.350 --> 00:13:56.610
as countering terrorism and deterring regional
aggression, we often diverge on how we go

00:13:56.610 --> 00:14:03.420
about securing those interests, the objectives
that we purse in search of those interests

00:14:03.420 --> 00:14:04.449
often diverge.

00:14:04.449 --> 00:14:06.889
I'm happy to dig into that throughout the
discussion.

00:14:06.889 --> 00:14:08.050
PICKERING: Thank you.

00:14:08.050 --> 00:14:09.050
Jim.

00:14:09.050 --> 00:14:12.859
JEFFREY: Tom, as you posed the question I
was thinking to myself, OK, the way to do

00:14:12.859 --> 00:14:18.209
that is to lay out, back in 2009 when I showed
up as ambassador, the three levels at which

00:14:18.209 --> 00:14:21.869
we approached Turkey diplomatically and politically.

00:14:21.869 --> 00:14:24.129
And then Steve laid all three of them out
perfectly.

00:14:24.129 --> 00:14:25.740
And I was really happy.

00:14:25.740 --> 00:14:28.760
It was even better than I was going to put
it.

00:14:28.760 --> 00:14:29.760
COOK: I'm sorry.

00:14:29.760 --> 00:14:30.760
(Laughter.)

00:14:30.760 --> 00:14:31.760
PICKERING: Stole your thunder.

00:14:31.760 --> 00:14:32.760
(Laughs.)

00:14:32.760 --> 00:14:34.750
JEFFREY: But let me then jump forward to where
we are now, because two of the three-trying

00:14:34.750 --> 00:14:39.869
to anchor Turkey in terms of institutions
and values into the West has somewhat faded,

00:14:39.869 --> 00:14:42.879
just like it's faded in Egypt, and Saudi Arabia,
and a lot of other places.

00:14:42.879 --> 00:14:46.439
And we all know why and the rationale for
it, whether we agree with it or not.

00:14:46.439 --> 00:14:54.329
And the idea of Turkey as a democratic political
Islam champion in the region, for even more

00:14:54.329 --> 00:14:57.579
clear reasons, that has kind of faded as well.

00:14:57.579 --> 00:15:03.160
And so we're left with the transactional getting
Turkey to work with us for our interest and

00:15:03.160 --> 00:15:04.160
mutual interest.

00:15:04.160 --> 00:15:05.160
And there are a lot of them.

00:15:05.160 --> 00:15:09.449
I'll inventory them very quickly, and then
point to the problem that will get back to

00:15:09.449 --> 00:15:11.050
Steve's three policies.

00:15:11.050 --> 00:15:18.059
First of all, if we're serious about dealing
with ISIS and dealing with the horrible situation

00:15:18.059 --> 00:15:26.199
in Syria, whatever our response is to the
expansionist moves of Russia and Iran in the

00:15:26.199 --> 00:15:32.540
region, if we want a solution in Cyprus which
for the first time since 2004 is actually

00:15:32.540 --> 00:15:37.429
close to being achieved, if we want to help
the further integration of Israel into the

00:15:37.429 --> 00:15:46.209
region by reopening cold relations with Turkey
and possibly expanding the gas in the Leviathan

00:15:46.209 --> 00:15:52.040
Field, the export of it, and more generally
if we want Turkey as a gas export hub from

00:15:52.040 --> 00:15:59.179
the Middle East and from the Caucasus as a
possible counter to Russia, and essentially

00:15:59.179 --> 00:16:04.119
if we want Turkey playing a stabilizing hand
in the Caucasus, where against we have problems

00:16:04.119 --> 00:16:08.459
right now with Nagorno-Karabakh, we have to
have that relationship.

00:16:08.459 --> 00:16:13.569
And as you said, Melissa, it very much is
based on security and military relations,

00:16:13.569 --> 00:16:15.019
as well as diplomacy.

00:16:15.019 --> 00:16:21.559
The problem is that we worry a lot about Erdogan's
ability in the long run to deliver it.

00:16:21.559 --> 00:16:23.410
He's a powerful guy.

00:16:23.410 --> 00:16:26.660
He's very solid in the saddle.

00:16:26.660 --> 00:16:29.279
In a lot of respects, he sort of looks like
Putin.

00:16:29.279 --> 00:16:34.680
The difference is, unlike Putin right now,
half the population is more or less adamantly

00:16:34.680 --> 00:16:36.119
opposed to him.

00:16:36.119 --> 00:16:42.319
And of that half, about half of them are totally
in a different philosophical an ideological

00:16:42.319 --> 00:16:43.809
worldview than Erdogan.

00:16:43.809 --> 00:16:46.480
So there's a great deal of internal tension.

00:16:46.480 --> 00:16:51.350
He is exploiting that and exacerbating it,
particularly in the way he's pursing the current

00:16:51.350 --> 00:16:57.600
campaign against the PKK, this Kurdish insurgent
group, that is having a terrible effect on

00:16:57.600 --> 00:17:00.230
the internal situation in Turkey.

00:17:00.230 --> 00:17:05.090
It's having a certain effect even on the economy,
which has been one of his very strong points,

00:17:05.090 --> 00:17:11.240
so that his ability to provide Turkey as a
platform for what he wants and what generally

00:17:11.240 --> 00:17:15.530
we both collectively want is under question
right now.

00:17:15.530 --> 00:17:21.589
And this raises the problem of for realpolitik
reasons, do we want to help him in some way

00:17:21.589 --> 00:17:28.831
to potentially change some of his internal
policies, not just for the fact that he is

00:17:28.831 --> 00:17:33.490
violating values that we hold dear, but the
fact that he's hurting Turkey's chances to

00:17:33.490 --> 00:17:36.260
play the role that he wants it to play, and
we certainly want it to play.

00:17:36.260 --> 00:17:37.260
Thank you.

00:17:37.260 --> 00:17:38.260
PICKERING: Thank you, Jim.

00:17:38.260 --> 00:17:43.610
I think what we've heard from the members
of the security council meeting is that our

00:17:43.610 --> 00:17:49.430
relationship with Turkey has not grown better,
arguably worse.

00:17:49.430 --> 00:17:55.180
Secondly, that it's a transactional relationship
now more than it ever had been.

00:17:55.180 --> 00:18:02.560
Thirdly, Jim has outlined a series of potential
carrots, although some of them strike me as

00:18:02.560 --> 00:18:04.430
more stick-like than carrot-like.

00:18:04.430 --> 00:18:05.940
(Laughter.)

00:18:05.940 --> 00:18:15.890
For example, Cyprus has had its own set of
very difficult arrangements in terms of arriving

00:18:15.890 --> 00:18:23.800
at a solution, which I suspect is going to
require more give than get on both sides,

00:18:23.800 --> 00:18:25.600
as they see this issue.

00:18:25.600 --> 00:18:29.770
And the startling and valuable thing is that
we have people on both sides who see this

00:18:29.770 --> 00:18:36.870
as a possibility rather than an exploitable
irritant in terms of getting there.

00:18:36.870 --> 00:18:47.610
Secondly, Israeli gas may be in some ways
consumable in the region as opposed to exportable,

00:18:47.610 --> 00:18:53.860
but what does Israel have to give to get its
gas out through Turkey, as opposed to what

00:18:53.860 --> 00:18:58.710
does Turkey have to give to get the gas exported
through Turkey?

00:18:58.710 --> 00:19:05.480
And that balance there is, of course, mirrored
in the Mavi Marmara problem of how and in

00:19:05.480 --> 00:19:12.590
what way do we take it the final steps between
Turkey and Israel, with the leaders on both

00:19:12.590 --> 00:19:19.390
sides seemingly have played blind-man's bluff
with baseball bats with each other in a way

00:19:19.390 --> 00:19:28.110
that has left the problem of a patch-up deeply
wounding in itself, perhaps, and not necessarily

00:19:28.110 --> 00:19:33.200
exploitable the way in which it had been in
the past, where the bedrock of Turkish-Israeli

00:19:33.200 --> 00:19:40.111
cooperation was heavily in the military sphere,
somewhat in the trade, but less, and as a

00:19:40.111 --> 00:19:45.510
result had some useful political connotations
for both of them, which is now seemingly gone

00:19:45.510 --> 00:19:50.500
up in smoke these many years and not be recoverable?

00:19:50.500 --> 00:19:56.910
The other problem is, what is that we could
do, if we were looking at way to tweak or

00:19:56.910 --> 00:20:05.840
shift our policy vis-�-vis the Kurds, that
might have some attraction to Erdogan and

00:20:05.840 --> 00:20:07.040
the Turks?

00:20:07.040 --> 00:20:08.350
What would they expect?

00:20:08.350 --> 00:20:15.210
What kind of arrangement should we be thinking
of with respect to the Russian problem?

00:20:15.210 --> 00:20:21.970
The Russians are playing a role in Syria where,
I suppose, you could say we need them as much

00:20:21.970 --> 00:20:23.960
as they need us.

00:20:23.960 --> 00:20:31.810
And so it is very difficult to see how extra
leverage might come out of the Syria negotiations.

00:20:31.810 --> 00:20:39.300
And our side, we see a serious problem with
what I would call Turkey's religious overhang,

00:20:39.300 --> 00:20:47.530
and less than serious attention either to
ISIS or perhaps the future of north Iraq.

00:20:47.530 --> 00:20:53.120
And we may, in a sense, have more leverage
working against us on that problem than we

00:20:53.120 --> 00:20:54.120
have coming for us.

00:20:54.120 --> 00:20:57.230
And so it's transactional certainly at this
stage.

00:20:57.230 --> 00:20:59.050
And that's not unimportant.

00:20:59.050 --> 00:21:07.590
Steve, what do you see as possible carrots
on our side to reevaluate this policy?

00:21:07.590 --> 00:21:14.850
COOK: It's an interesting question, because
it strikes me that in each one of these policy

00:21:14.850 --> 00:21:19.120
areas that you've outlined, or these problems
that you've outlined, the way in which either

00:21:19.120 --> 00:21:26.800
the Turks, or their partners, or their adversaries
define the issue makes it difficult for the

00:21:26.800 --> 00:21:31.810
United States to gain some leverage or offer
carrots or sticks in the conflict.

00:21:31.810 --> 00:21:36.250
That to suggest that they define these conflicts
in existential terms.

00:21:36.250 --> 00:21:44.090
So how do you-how do you move the Turks with
regard to our position on the Kurds?

00:21:44.090 --> 00:21:49.040
It's very, very hard to do that, given the
fact that we are working with the PYD and

00:21:49.040 --> 00:21:55.360
YPG, which the Turks clearly believe to be
closely associated with the PKK.

00:21:55.360 --> 00:21:59.690
What are we to do, given the fact that the
YPG seems to be more effective than other

00:21:59.690 --> 00:22:02.840
friends on the ground against the Islamic
State, and that's what we have identified

00:22:02.840 --> 00:22:04.030
as our priority?

00:22:04.030 --> 00:22:08.711
We would have to get the Turks to step up
in the first against the Islamic State, but

00:22:08.711 --> 00:22:12.380
they prioritize their fight as a fight against
the Kurds.

00:22:12.380 --> 00:22:16.440
So it makes it extraordinarily difficult to
find how we have leverage in this kind of

00:22:16.440 --> 00:22:17.440
situation.

00:22:17.440 --> 00:22:21.300
I think actually, as difficult as it may be,
and I think you probably know this first hand,

00:22:21.300 --> 00:22:27.740
Tom, the area where we might have some sticks
and carrots is in the Turkey-Israel relationship.

00:22:27.740 --> 00:22:31.660
Now, I'm on record as being extraordinarily
skeptical about this.

00:22:31.660 --> 00:22:36.190
One, in thinking about the man, Erdogan, which
I think we do too much of and we don't think

00:22:36.190 --> 00:22:40.340
enough about his constituency and how he has
actually transformed Turkey in a way that

00:22:40.340 --> 00:22:45.210
is quite popular for at least half the population.

00:22:45.210 --> 00:22:50.150
It's hard to think, though, that Erdogan after
the Mavi Marmara, after a couple wars in the

00:22:50.150 --> 00:22:54.510
Gaza Strip, after everything that he has said,
walking this back in a way that they can go

00:22:54.510 --> 00:22:58.730
back to some kind of business as usual, in
the same way it seems hard, despite the fact

00:22:58.730 --> 00:23:02.720
that, you know, bureaucrats and security people
in both Israel and Turkey kind of want to

00:23:02.720 --> 00:23:07.570
have this relationship, it seems hard in Israel
for them to politically do this.

00:23:07.570 --> 00:23:10.390
Now, the problem now is-it's not really a
problem.

00:23:10.390 --> 00:23:18.730
Where we do have some leverage is the Russian-Turkey
relationship is a way to kind of push the

00:23:18.730 --> 00:23:20.210
Turks in the direction of the Israelis.

00:23:20.210 --> 00:23:21.780
That's something that we're already seeing.

00:23:21.780 --> 00:23:28.620
The Israelis, with the big gas find off of
Egypt that Eni found last year.

00:23:28.620 --> 00:23:31.830
The Egyptians aren't going to need Israeli
gas.

00:23:31.830 --> 00:23:36.320
The Egyptians may well go back to sending
gas to the Jordanians, if they can get control

00:23:36.320 --> 00:23:37.680
of the situation in the Sinai.

00:23:37.680 --> 00:23:40.020
Where are the Israelis going to sell their
gas?

00:23:40.020 --> 00:23:43.880
And the most logical place for them to sell
their gas through is Turkey.

00:23:43.880 --> 00:23:51.390
Now, for all of the deep-sixing of the Israel-Turkey
relationship, the one place where things are

00:23:51.390 --> 00:23:55.410
actually quite good is on that business-to-business,
trade-to-trade relationship.

00:23:55.410 --> 00:23:57.310
Israeli business and Turkish business has
flourished.

00:23:57.310 --> 00:24:03.400
The airline that has the most departures from
Tel Aviv is not El Al, it's Turkish Airlines.

00:24:03.400 --> 00:24:08.530
So there is a way in which the United States-and
I imagine that we are doing this and we should

00:24:08.530 --> 00:24:12.820
continue to do this-is kind of-it's never
going to go back to where it was, but this

00:24:12.820 --> 00:24:17.350
is a place where if we want to think about
reintegrating Turkey into the region, whose

00:24:17.350 --> 00:24:22.592
entire strategic position has collapsed, one
of the ways to do it is with Israel, whose

00:24:22.592 --> 00:24:28.320
strategic position in the region has never
been greater, and a way of kind of tying these

00:24:28.320 --> 00:24:31.120
countries up tighter is through this energy
thing.

00:24:31.120 --> 00:24:36.710
Other than that, all these other issues that
you've outlined seem to me very, very hard

00:24:36.710 --> 00:24:40.820
for us to generate any kind of significant
leverage to kind of compel the Turks to do

00:24:40.820 --> 00:24:43.310
something, because of the way in which they
define their conflicts.

00:24:43.310 --> 00:24:49.320
PICKERING: Let me suggest something that may
come out of your thoughts as an idea we ought

00:24:49.320 --> 00:24:51.560
to pursue further.

00:24:51.560 --> 00:24:59.200
And that's the question of whether the overt
patch up on the business side, Turkey to Israel,

00:24:59.200 --> 00:25:02.760
shouldn't be the flagship, the public face.

00:25:02.760 --> 00:25:07.050
But the Israelis and the Turks over the years
have learned how to cooperate in the military

00:25:07.050 --> 00:25:13.260
sphere in ways that have eluded and indeed
subverted publicity.

00:25:13.260 --> 00:25:19.130
And I think it might be useful to begin to
suggest-Erdogan I think will be the most difficult

00:25:19.130 --> 00:25:26.930
in this, even though he has perhaps the most
to gain in terms of Israeli technology and

00:25:26.930 --> 00:25:31.160
Israeli tactical capacities and things of
that sort.

00:25:31.160 --> 00:25:35.380
And he knows over the history, say, of the
Turkish Air Force that they've gained a great

00:25:35.380 --> 00:25:42.300
deal through cooperation with the Israelis,
which in many ways is still there to be maintained

00:25:42.300 --> 00:25:43.900
and pushed ahead.

00:25:43.900 --> 00:25:49.560
So that would be something that one would
want to investigate as a way ahead.

00:25:49.560 --> 00:25:56.790
How and what way we could use that as a carrot,
and what we would expect to get in return

00:25:56.790 --> 00:25:59.250
is a little harder to say.

00:25:59.250 --> 00:26:04.670
The second question I have, and Jim maybe
you would address this a little bit, and Melissa,

00:26:04.670 --> 00:26:09.680
talk a little bit about the Israeli-Turkey
relationship when we come around to you, but

00:26:09.680 --> 00:26:15.930
Jim take a look at the question of whether
there is something that we could do in the

00:26:15.930 --> 00:26:20.340
longer run on the Kurdish side politically.

00:26:20.340 --> 00:26:27.960
I would think that with the significant amount
of influence we have in Iraqi Kurdistan, and

00:26:27.960 --> 00:26:37.800
the relationships might be able to build something
a little more solid in Kurdish-Turkish relationships,

00:26:37.800 --> 00:26:41.220
which the Turkish side might find useful.

00:26:41.220 --> 00:26:46.390
They already dominate pieces of that relationship,
sometimes militarily.

00:26:46.390 --> 00:26:53.560
But moving it out of that field, could one
look at a series of reciprocal commitments

00:26:53.560 --> 00:27:00.330
to non-action or action that might in some
ways help to strengthen Turkish confidence

00:27:00.330 --> 00:27:06.730
that essentially pseudo-independence in Iraqi
Kurdistan was not a dagger pointed at the

00:27:06.730 --> 00:27:14.370
heart of Turkey, but in fact a set of relationships
and allies where they could work both with

00:27:14.370 --> 00:27:22.090
the folks in Syrian Kurdistan, but also stay
out of Turkish Kurdistan, perhaps find useful

00:27:22.090 --> 00:27:24.490
ways to try to influence people there.

00:27:24.490 --> 00:27:28.700
It's a lot better to have friends in the Kurdish
world, if you have a Kurdish problem, than

00:27:28.700 --> 00:27:29.830
it is to have enemies.

00:27:29.830 --> 00:27:31.340
Is there something there?

00:27:31.340 --> 00:27:37.760
And then, finally, I think of course NATO
and Article 5 are not irrelevant with respect

00:27:37.760 --> 00:27:39.110
to Russia.

00:27:39.110 --> 00:27:46.640
On the other hand, I'm not sure you can sell
Article 5 two, three times a week to the Turks

00:27:46.640 --> 00:27:48.430
for something else.

00:27:48.430 --> 00:27:50.630
And then the question would be, what would
we like?

00:27:50.630 --> 00:27:57.050
I would put a fairly high priority, particularly
for something like the Kurdish arrangement,

00:27:57.050 --> 00:28:01.190
on a better Turkish effort on two things.

00:28:01.190 --> 00:28:07.080
We didn't discuss it at length, but refugees-that
is, keeping refugees perhaps in some kind

00:28:07.080 --> 00:28:12.170
of safe area in northern Syria where Turkey
would have to have some responsibility, but

00:28:12.170 --> 00:28:13.170
others would as well.

00:28:13.170 --> 00:28:18.320
There's no use gathering refugees who are
going to be decimated by barrel bombs.

00:28:18.320 --> 00:28:23.950
Or in southern Turkey, which is a more, put
it this way, trenchant requirement on Turkey

00:28:23.950 --> 00:28:25.309
to deal with.

00:28:25.309 --> 00:28:27.030
But that might be useful.

00:28:27.030 --> 00:28:28.030
And that would be helpful.

00:28:28.030 --> 00:28:34.210
The second is a tighter seal on the Turkish
border, as we know that it has been, and in

00:28:34.210 --> 00:28:40.730
fact used by some of our Gulf friends occasionally,
to support what we would call marginally interesting

00:28:40.730 --> 00:28:43.210
Syrian opposition organizations.

00:28:43.210 --> 00:28:51.581
JEFFREY: Tom, on your last couple of points,
it's somewhat frustrating because on the three

00:28:51.581 --> 00:29:03.930
security problems Turkey sees to itself-PKK/PYD,
the Kurdish problem; the ISIS problem, of

00:29:03.930 --> 00:29:10.720
course; and the Syrian government-we at a
certain level of abstraction are kind of in

00:29:10.720 --> 00:29:14.960
agreement that they're all three problems,
and you know the boilerplate.

00:29:14.960 --> 00:29:19.650
The problem is, how we rack and stack those
three, and the priorities we give and the

00:29:19.650 --> 00:29:25.510
intensity of it differs between the two of
us at the detail level, which is the only

00:29:25.510 --> 00:29:26.510
level that really counts.

00:29:26.510 --> 00:29:32.210
I'm reminded of, as you'll remember, how we
were all frustrated in Bosnia from '91 to

00:29:32.210 --> 00:29:37.970
'95, because that was the relationship between
us and any one of five or six other players.

00:29:37.970 --> 00:29:42.070
We all wanted in the end to be the same place,
but how to get there we didn't-and then finally,

00:29:42.070 --> 00:29:45.230
we kind of all found the lowest common denominator.

00:29:45.230 --> 00:29:46.250
That's what we need.

00:29:46.250 --> 00:29:51.280
It starts, as you said, with the Kurds.

00:29:51.280 --> 00:29:57.790
We need-and I think the reason for the 250
special forces going into Syria is largely

00:29:57.790 --> 00:29:58.790
for that reason.

00:29:58.790 --> 00:30:05.809
We are looking for people to take on ISIS,
other than the Syrian Kurds, because they

00:30:05.809 --> 00:30:11.680
now have reached the outer limits of the Kurdish
areas, and to put them into Arab areas, particularly

00:30:11.680 --> 00:30:17.900
without some Arab-well, they have some-but
more Arab allies and probably more American

00:30:17.900 --> 00:30:20.130
eyes on is particularly worrisome.

00:30:20.130 --> 00:30:25.110
I think that that's an area where we can start
approaching them and saying: Look, our relationship

00:30:25.110 --> 00:30:31.700
with the PYD, this is, much as you don't trust
us-and they don't trust us-but still, this

00:30:31.700 --> 00:30:33.600
is how far we're going to go.

00:30:33.600 --> 00:30:34.750
It has physical limits.

00:30:34.750 --> 00:30:38.900
We don't go across the Euphrates, other than
the one place we did go across the Euphrates,

00:30:38.900 --> 00:30:43.630
and there's going to be real limits to this.

00:30:43.630 --> 00:30:49.000
Secondly, with the PKK, there behind closed
doors we have been very, very helpful in all

00:30:49.000 --> 00:30:52.980
kinds of very sensitive ways on fighting the
PKK over the years.

00:30:52.980 --> 00:30:59.620
We need to tell Erdogan that, without letting
the cat out of the bag, which we know is that

00:30:59.620 --> 00:31:11.270
he's using this in part for domestic political
almost xenophobic rousing the masses, if you

00:31:11.270 --> 00:31:12.270
will.

00:31:12.270 --> 00:31:14.490
But we have to tell him that this is not going
to turn out well.

00:31:14.490 --> 00:31:16.870
There are ways that we can work with him.

00:31:16.870 --> 00:31:18.850
There's ways that we can work with Barzani.

00:31:18.850 --> 00:31:20.250
So that would be the first thing.

00:31:20.250 --> 00:31:25.680
On the Syrian threat, again, something like
a safe zone.

00:31:25.680 --> 00:31:29.960
Anything that looks like we're not going to
walk away from the Syrian problem and leave

00:31:29.960 --> 00:31:37.050
Ankara to face Assad backed by the Russians
and the Iranians will be very helpful.

00:31:37.050 --> 00:31:40.679
That's why pulling out those Patriots was
symbolic, but it really hurt.

00:31:40.679 --> 00:31:46.660
And then the last one, against ISIS, again,
they're about two-thirds there.

00:31:46.660 --> 00:31:52.590
It's more a question of priority than some
kid of sneaky backroom relationship with them.

00:31:52.590 --> 00:31:54.780
They have done a lot to cut off the border.

00:31:54.780 --> 00:32:00.050
We're seeing very reduced flows of foreign
fighters in, for many reasons, including the

00:32:00.050 --> 00:32:01.200
Turks' actions.

00:32:01.200 --> 00:32:04.760
So you can see a way to do all three of those
things.

00:32:04.760 --> 00:32:11.890
What they would want is us to lay out a comprehensive
strategy-what I easily did because I'm not

00:32:11.890 --> 00:32:16.000
in government, it's something we're doing
in a lot of detail-on the one hand.

00:32:16.000 --> 00:32:22.260
And then secondly, Erdogan would have to move
away from his more extreme exploitation of

00:32:22.260 --> 00:32:26.100
these issues, particularly the Kurdish one,
from domestic consumption.

00:32:26.100 --> 00:32:32.430
We would have to move a little bit away from
our very expansive American, you know, what

00:32:32.430 --> 00:32:35.770
we want the region to look like and what we
want to happen and not happen.

00:32:35.770 --> 00:32:37.790
ISIS goes away and all this.

00:32:37.790 --> 00:32:40.320
While putting in a minimum American presence.

00:32:40.320 --> 00:32:42.150
There's got to be a closing of the gap.

00:32:42.150 --> 00:32:43.240
And I think it's possible.

00:32:43.240 --> 00:32:44.470
PICKERING: Thanks, Jim.

00:32:44.470 --> 00:32:54.390
Melissa, talk a little bit about your reactions
to the potential for maybe more quiet Turkish-Israeli

00:32:54.390 --> 00:32:56.100
resumption of military cooperation.

00:32:56.100 --> 00:33:00.650
And what is it that we see, because it's always
there.

00:33:00.650 --> 00:33:07.800
What's on the ledger that Turkey wants from
us on the military side in a transactional

00:33:07.800 --> 00:33:13.809
set of relationships that could be used to
sweeten, or improve, or lever the relationship?

00:33:13.809 --> 00:33:14.809
DALTON: Great.

00:33:14.809 --> 00:33:15.809
Thanks.

00:33:15.809 --> 00:33:17.950
Picking up those threads, and then I'll add
in a couple of others.

00:33:17.950 --> 00:33:25.429
On Israel-Turkey, from the United States perspective,
having two allies at the northern and southern

00:33:25.429 --> 00:33:32.220
ends of the Syria-Iraq mess kind of anchoring
the situation would be incredibly helpful.

00:33:32.220 --> 00:33:34.350
Right now there are tensions with Turkey.

00:33:34.350 --> 00:33:36.060
There are arguably also tensions with Israel.

00:33:36.060 --> 00:33:41.780
So shoring up the bilateral relationship between
the two, and then also working on our bilateral

00:33:41.780 --> 00:33:45.500
relationships with both countries I think
would be incredibly advantageous.

00:33:45.500 --> 00:33:53.070
And I think the hook that Ambassador Pickering
mentioned of defense technology cooperation

00:33:53.070 --> 00:33:58.950
between the Israelis and the Turks is definitely
worth pursuing.

00:33:58.950 --> 00:34:06.800
In terms of other carrots that we might offer
from the military defense basket, I think

00:34:06.800 --> 00:34:14.659
looking more broadly at the Russian challenge,
and particularly the Black Sea region, there's

00:34:14.659 --> 00:34:21.369
some area for enhanced cooperation that might
have advantageous effects for the overall

00:34:21.369 --> 00:34:28.999
bilateral and NATO relationship with Turkey,
given the stepped up Russian aggression in

00:34:28.999 --> 00:34:38.269
that region, particularly in maritime security
cooperation, intelligence, ISR sharing, and

00:34:38.269 --> 00:34:41.359
combined exercises that we might do in the
region.

00:34:41.359 --> 00:34:47.919
And enhancing those overtime I think would
both increase our deterrence vis-�-vis Russia,

00:34:47.919 --> 00:34:53.609
and help us achieve common objectives that
NATO, including the Turks and the United States,

00:34:53.609 --> 00:35:01.430
have, but also have second-order effects for
how the Russians perceive NATO, and Turkey,

00:35:01.430 --> 00:35:05.390
again, included in that, and its effects on
the southern region.

00:35:05.390 --> 00:35:12.289
And I think having a more holistic picture
of our defense engagement in the broader region

00:35:12.289 --> 00:35:16.690
would be quite helpful going forward.

00:35:16.690 --> 00:35:19.619
On the Kurds, if I may, I think-
PICKERING: Let me just do one thing.

00:35:19.619 --> 00:35:20.619
DALTON: Yeah.

00:35:20.619 --> 00:35:21.720
PICKERING: A military question for you-two
military questions.

00:35:21.720 --> 00:35:22.720
DALTON: Yes.

00:35:22.720 --> 00:35:27.609
PICKERING: What would be wrong with suggesting
that since the Israelis have already committed

00:35:27.609 --> 00:35:35.599
themselves to the Russians, to take out any
equipment shipped to Assad that poses any

00:35:35.599 --> 00:35:38.450
threat to their air supremacy.

00:35:38.450 --> 00:35:44.650
What about translating that to something that
would also relate to Turkish interests, in

00:35:44.650 --> 00:35:45.859
one way or another?

00:35:45.859 --> 00:35:49.329
Is there a piece of that that might be useful?

00:35:49.329 --> 00:35:59.260
And then, I think-the second question, I think,
is U.S.-Turkey.

00:35:59.260 --> 00:36:05.380
And I think there the issue is, are there
particular items of equipment that they want?

00:36:05.380 --> 00:36:11.980
Is, for example, also an Israeli possibility
that we could help them link their two air

00:36:11.980 --> 00:36:17.660
defense perimeters, so that they could see
what the Turks see and the Turks could see

00:36:17.660 --> 00:36:24.650
what the Israelis see, as an additional effort
to provide security and certainty about the

00:36:24.650 --> 00:36:28.000
air picture that clearly both of them are
concerned about.

00:36:28.000 --> 00:36:32.990
DALTON: I do think that those are two right
options to explore.

00:36:32.990 --> 00:36:40.900
On the interdiction piece, at minimum having
a better common operating picture with not

00:36:40.900 --> 00:36:47.539
only the Israelis but with the Turks sharing
across the Mediterranean and to the south,

00:36:47.539 --> 00:36:53.069
that there's more that we could do to help
bring the Turks into that picture, allow them

00:36:53.069 --> 00:36:57.109
to have better eyes on the situation, and
then also pass information to us.

00:36:57.109 --> 00:37:00.559
PICKERING: Well, the Turks can see into Iraq
and to Iran, which the Israelis would like

00:37:00.559 --> 00:37:03.099
to see, if they don't already.

00:37:03.099 --> 00:37:04.470
DALTON: Right, exactly.

00:37:04.470 --> 00:37:05.970
So there's that element of it.

00:37:05.970 --> 00:37:11.089
In terms of bilateral defense cooperation
with the Turks, between the United States

00:37:11.089 --> 00:37:17.609
and Turkey, I think the air defense element
of it, stepping up their capabilities is particularly

00:37:17.609 --> 00:37:26.670
top of mind given the threat to Kilis right
now, and the rocket attacks that Turkey is

00:37:26.670 --> 00:37:27.670
suffering from.

00:37:27.670 --> 00:37:32.519
And actually, some lessons learned to be garnered
from Israel in that sense.

00:37:32.519 --> 00:37:37.759
It is, in fact, Katyushas that are being launched
into Kilis.

00:37:37.759 --> 00:37:42.539
The Israelis, of course, have Iron Dome, which,
you know, at the moment there are prospects

00:37:42.539 --> 00:37:48.260
to develop that kind of capability with Turkey,
but, you know, certain tactics, techniques,

00:37:48.260 --> 00:37:53.050
and procedures that the Israelis use to combat
that threat could be shared with the Turks.

00:37:53.050 --> 00:38:00.019
And there may be specific air defense systems
that the United States might be able to provide

00:38:00.019 --> 00:38:03.440
to the Turks over the next several years.

00:38:03.440 --> 00:38:09.430
And the United States actually just this week
announced that we will be deploying HIMARS,

00:38:09.430 --> 00:38:14.779
a high altitude artillery rocket system, into
southern Turkey, as well as in Iraq, to combat

00:38:14.779 --> 00:38:16.499
the ISIS artillery threat.

00:38:16.499 --> 00:38:23.590
PICKERING: Would it be also of potential interest
to secure, if they don't have it already,

00:38:23.590 --> 00:38:27.640
kind of an air picture link between Iraq and
Turkey at this stage?

00:38:27.640 --> 00:38:29.730
DALTON: Sure.

00:38:29.730 --> 00:38:37.089
In the 2003 Iraq War, which sort of blends
together with the current one, but to distinguish,

00:38:37.089 --> 00:38:44.309
there was, as Ambassador Jeffrey alluded to,
significant military intelligence cooperation

00:38:44.309 --> 00:38:49.480
on kind of the southern border of Turkey,
northern border of Iraq.

00:38:49.480 --> 00:38:55.450
And so stepping up those intelligence and
operations fusion efforts I think would be

00:38:55.450 --> 00:38:56.450
quite useful.

00:38:56.450 --> 00:39:00.589
PICKERING: And Turkey as an essential link
between Israel and the picture would be something

00:39:00.589 --> 00:39:02.630
useful to offer there.

00:39:02.630 --> 00:39:04.720
DALTON: Absolutely, absolutely, to see the
full region.

00:39:04.720 --> 00:39:05.720
PICKERING: The full region.

00:39:05.720 --> 00:39:06.720
DALTON: Yeah.

00:39:06.720 --> 00:39:09.619
PICKERING: In any event, these are some ideas.

00:39:09.619 --> 00:39:14.089
We're going to do one brief round with the
three of you.

00:39:14.089 --> 00:39:20.250
What else should we be thinking about that
we're not thinking about in the policy specifics

00:39:20.250 --> 00:39:21.250
here?

00:39:21.250 --> 00:39:25.269
I think the framework we're working under
has changed a little bit, but the framework

00:39:25.269 --> 00:39:33.430
is really-the pillar of the framework is what
can we do to preserve Turkish help and cooperation,

00:39:33.430 --> 00:39:34.650
and improve it?

00:39:34.650 --> 00:39:36.410
And that seems to be the general direction.

00:39:36.410 --> 00:39:39.940
Steve, I've ignored you for a while, so what
can you give us?

00:39:39.940 --> 00:39:43.930
What advice or thoughts, or what should we
be looking at here?

00:39:43.930 --> 00:39:49.700
Things that you know bother the Turks that
maybe in one way or another we have an opportunity

00:39:49.700 --> 00:39:52.010
to turn into a transactional help.

00:39:52.010 --> 00:39:56.279
COOK: Well, the things that I know that bother
the Turks, I wonder whether we're really actually

00:39:56.279 --> 00:39:59.210
willing to give on those issues, and that's
specifically the-

00:39:59.210 --> 00:40:01.800
PICKERING: Sure, but my view is let's test
all assumptions here at the NSC.

00:40:01.800 --> 00:40:02.800
COOK: Right.

00:40:02.800 --> 00:40:06.010
And I think that one of the things that we-that
is a transactional relationship, and this

00:40:06.010 --> 00:40:12.650
is going to make some people angry, but is
that if, as Ambassador Jeffrey pointed out,

00:40:12.650 --> 00:40:16.440
the Turks are two-thirds of the way there
on the Islamic State, then we can ease off

00:40:16.440 --> 00:40:19.809
on the YPG and the PYD.

00:40:19.809 --> 00:40:24.809
And that is something that we traded over
the course of many years many times.

00:40:24.809 --> 00:40:30.510
It's not trading the Iraqi Kurds, which are
inching ever closer towards independence,

00:40:30.510 --> 00:40:33.759
which is something that I think is going to
bother the Turks so much.

00:40:33.759 --> 00:40:37.559
So we could trade the YPG/PYD for greater
Turkish cooperation.

00:40:37.559 --> 00:40:41.400
I think that we have to have a conversation,
as Ambassador Jeffrey said, about what an

00:40:41.400 --> 00:40:44.849
actual strategy combatting the Islamic State
in Syria means.

00:40:44.849 --> 00:40:48.759
It doesn't mean, necessarily, marching an
American army onto Damascus, which is what

00:40:48.759 --> 00:40:53.140
I think the Turkish preference would be.

00:40:53.140 --> 00:40:56.180
We will get nowhere with them on domestic
politics, so I don't think we should even

00:40:56.180 --> 00:40:57.180
bring it up.

00:40:57.180 --> 00:41:03.799
And I think to the extent-and this is important
in terms of trading-is that if we define what

00:41:03.799 --> 00:41:08.380
is important about Turkey as what's going
on around Turkey, then not hammering them

00:41:08.380 --> 00:41:13.790
on the very clear authoritarian turn in Turkish
politics is a wise thing, because when we

00:41:13.790 --> 00:41:20.960
do that, and I know there's tremendous concern,
that when we do that they will, in a fit of

00:41:20.960 --> 00:41:25.180
pique and anger, pull back from the things
that we think that they're doing that is important

00:41:25.180 --> 00:41:26.180
for us.

00:41:26.180 --> 00:41:32.030
So perhaps we should say-as difficult as that
is to say as a person with values-to say something

00:41:32.030 --> 00:41:33.030
like that.

00:41:33.030 --> 00:41:35.330
Those are really the places-and of course,
as I mentioned before, I think that there

00:41:35.330 --> 00:41:40.920
are greater options now on the Israel front,
as politically difficult as it is for both

00:41:40.920 --> 00:41:46.810
Turkish and Israeli politicians to kind of
swallow hard and move forward here, I think

00:41:46.810 --> 00:41:52.081
that there are some things, given the changes
around the region, for us to kind of push

00:41:52.081 --> 00:41:55.570
the Turks in the direction of the Israelis
and the Israelis in the direction of the Turks

00:41:55.570 --> 00:41:58.390
that will benefit all of us.

00:41:58.390 --> 00:42:02.640
But my sense is that it's going to be less-it'll
be a quiet conversation about the kinds of

00:42:02.640 --> 00:42:06.489
things that Elizabeth is talking about-Melissa.

00:42:06.489 --> 00:42:09.180
Did I call you Elizabeth?

00:42:09.180 --> 00:42:10.230
DALTON: It's OK.

00:42:10.230 --> 00:42:11.230
It's a nice name.

00:42:11.230 --> 00:42:12.230
COOK: I'm sorry.

00:42:12.230 --> 00:42:13.230
I'm sorry.

00:42:13.230 --> 00:42:14.230
I knew it as soon as I said it.

00:42:14.230 --> 00:42:15.230
As soon as I said I knew-so anyway, my apologies.

00:42:15.230 --> 00:42:16.230
DALTON: It's fine.

00:42:16.230 --> 00:42:18.079
COOK: And more on that kind of commercial
front.

00:42:18.079 --> 00:42:23.880
And the gas stuff I think is right the same
and forward for all of the players there.

00:42:23.880 --> 00:42:24.979
It's the Turks.

00:42:24.979 --> 00:42:26.530
It's the Israelis.

00:42:26.530 --> 00:42:27.530
It's the Cypriots.

00:42:27.530 --> 00:42:28.530
It's the Greeks.

00:42:28.530 --> 00:42:29.530
And, quite frankly, it's also the Egyptians.

00:42:29.530 --> 00:42:32.430
And we hit all those, that would be-that would
change things, I think.

00:42:32.430 --> 00:42:38.210
PICKERING: Let me pick up on two other pieces
here, one related to what you just said.

00:42:38.210 --> 00:42:44.690
Cyprus is not going to be an easy course,
but it would settle a problem that has been

00:42:44.690 --> 00:42:49.799
an irritant for Turkey for a long time, despite
the fact that Turkey and Greece played a mutually

00:42:49.799 --> 00:42:52.519
irritating role in getting there.

00:42:52.519 --> 00:42:55.359
And getting that off the picture would be
helpful.

00:42:55.359 --> 00:43:01.769
Are there things that we could think of, maybe
quiet things for a change, that would help

00:43:01.769 --> 00:43:07.160
to facilitate the Cyprus workout, that we
could get some credit for the Turks for?

00:43:07.160 --> 00:43:10.390
And I think that might be something we think
about.

00:43:10.390 --> 00:43:15.630
The bigger question, and I'll ask Jim and
Melissa too to comment on this, is Kurdish

00:43:15.630 --> 00:43:19.109
policy on the part of the U.S.

00:43:19.109 --> 00:43:26.650
We clearly have in one way or another tolerated,
if not directly supported, the creation of

00:43:26.650 --> 00:43:32.859
wider independence for the Kurds in northern
Iraq, for a whole series of reasons.

00:43:32.859 --> 00:43:41.069
Is it time for us to have one of those, put
it this way, hail Mary conversations with

00:43:41.069 --> 00:43:46.440
the Kurds-I didn't want to say come to Jesus
conversation, because that's a different story-(laughter)-with

00:43:46.440 --> 00:43:50.410
the Kurds about their long-term future?

00:43:50.410 --> 00:43:56.670
The values of a long-term future as a fully
autonomous piece of Iraq which doesn't take

00:43:56.670 --> 00:44:00.920
them over the lines they'd like to cross,
but can't pay for, if I could put it this

00:44:00.920 --> 00:44:02.849
way, or can't stand the heat on.

00:44:02.849 --> 00:44:04.430
Is that something that's useful?

00:44:04.430 --> 00:44:10.160
And is that conundrum, or that approach, or
that paradigm something that can be usefully

00:44:10.160 --> 00:44:11.839
reassuring to the Turks?

00:44:11.839 --> 00:44:14.359
JEFFREY: Yeah.,
PICKERING: Go ahead.

00:44:14.359 --> 00:44:18.680
JEFFREY: Let me tackle that, but back into
it.

00:44:18.680 --> 00:44:23.079
What the Turks would like from us, and it
fits with the Kurdish thing, is two things.

00:44:23.079 --> 00:44:29.789
At the strategic level, again, the Kurds will
play a big role in this, is-or the Kurdish

00:44:29.789 --> 00:44:37.069
question-what is America's vision or role
in the Middle East in terms of overall American

00:44:37.069 --> 00:44:38.150
foreign policy?

00:44:38.150 --> 00:44:40.690
And flowing from that is things they want
to-

00:44:40.690 --> 00:44:42.349
PICKERING: Well, that's going to be our next
NSC meeting.

00:44:42.349 --> 00:44:43.349
(Laughter.)

00:44:43.349 --> 00:44:44.349
JEFFREY: Yeah, OK.

00:44:44.349 --> 00:44:51.599
But it gets to things like how important is
it for us to maintain the current state system

00:44:51.599 --> 00:44:53.630
in the Middle East and the current borders?

00:44:53.630 --> 00:45:00.190
From that then flows how do we deal with the
Kurds-and I'm a big proponent-and I'm not

00:45:00.190 --> 00:45:05.710
the entire NSC or the U.S. government or the
next government so I'll just leave my points

00:45:05.710 --> 00:45:07.880
to one side-
PICKERING: Now, hang on, you won't be fired

00:45:07.880 --> 00:45:08.880
today.

00:45:08.880 --> 00:45:09.880
(Laughs.)

00:45:09.880 --> 00:45:10.880
JEFFREY: OK.

00:45:10.880 --> 00:45:15.269
But we do need to figure out, OK, how does
all this play?

00:45:15.269 --> 00:45:24.050
And what are the consequences of ignoring
the Kurdish northern Iraq issue or embracing-and

00:45:24.050 --> 00:45:28.540
there are lots of people in this town who
would like to embrace it-a kind of independence,

00:45:28.540 --> 00:45:31.329
or reigning them in?

00:45:31.329 --> 00:45:34.779
And that has to fit into something larger,
or it won't have legs.

00:45:34.779 --> 00:45:38.599
And the Turks know that, as do the Kurds.

00:45:38.599 --> 00:45:47.940
And I think that if we can flow from our basic
principles our specific responses and various

00:45:47.940 --> 00:45:51.799
things, this will help us be more predictable
to the Turks, because one of the things that

00:45:51.799 --> 00:45:57.990
they accuse us of is not being predictable
and going back and forth on various things.

00:45:57.990 --> 00:45:59.999
And there's a lot to be said for that argument.

00:45:59.999 --> 00:46:05.310
So more predictability in the relationship
and a more specific-otherwise, they'll think

00:46:05.310 --> 00:46:11.009
the worse of every relationship we have with
any Kurd, that it's a way to pressure them,

00:46:11.009 --> 00:46:12.900
that we're always out to get them.

00:46:12.900 --> 00:46:16.289
And there are ways to reassure them, but they
take an awful lot of work.

00:46:16.289 --> 00:46:25.130
PICKERING: And that reassurance might at some
point even take the form of some serious Iraqi

00:46:25.130 --> 00:46:27.309
Kurdish conversations with the Turks.

00:46:27.309 --> 00:46:28.309
JEFFREY: Exactly.

00:46:28.309 --> 00:46:31.200
The relationship there is actually quite good.

00:46:31.200 --> 00:46:32.200
PICKERING: Quite good?

00:46:32.200 --> 00:46:34.930
JEFFREY: But it's-
PICKERING: But we have to take out of the

00:46:34.930 --> 00:46:37.440
closet the issues that aren't easily addressed.

00:46:37.440 --> 00:46:40.339
And we have to prepare the closet before we
do that.

00:46:40.339 --> 00:46:44.210
JEFFREY: The Turks do not want to see an independent
northern Iraq, period.

00:46:44.210 --> 00:46:45.210
PICKERING: Yeah.

00:46:45.210 --> 00:46:48.150
Well, ladies and gentlemen, you've all joined
the meeting.

00:46:48.150 --> 00:46:49.809
And you've heard it.

00:46:49.809 --> 00:46:51.119
And I'll just go around.

00:46:51.119 --> 00:46:55.479
Is there-Sam, do you want to pick the people,
for any reason?

00:46:55.479 --> 00:46:57.519
They're all my friends, so it's OK.

00:46:57.519 --> 00:46:58.519
(Laughter.)

00:46:58.519 --> 00:46:59.519
Please right here.

00:46:59.519 --> 00:47:00.820
And the microphone will come to you.

00:47:00.820 --> 00:47:03.009
Please tell us who you are and your affiliation.

00:47:03.009 --> 00:47:04.009
Q: Yeah.

00:47:04.009 --> 00:47:07.560
Marisa Lino, Northrop Grumman, formerly of
State.

00:47:07.560 --> 00:47:13.140
I would like to go back to one of the three
pillars that Steve mentioned, and that is

00:47:13.140 --> 00:47:17.089
anchoring Turkey in the West.

00:47:17.089 --> 00:47:22.930
Everybody knows the long and painful history
of Turkey with the EU, which has run hot and

00:47:22.930 --> 00:47:24.520
cold on both sides.

00:47:24.520 --> 00:47:30.430
And it seems like in the last decade it's
run cold on the Turkish side, except most

00:47:30.430 --> 00:47:37.319
recently with this whole question of the visas
and so forth, it seems like Turkey's, to me,

00:47:37.319 --> 00:47:42.469
somewhat illogically, turning again hot on
becoming part of the EU.

00:47:42.469 --> 00:47:46.990
And I'm curious if you agree with that or
not, and if so, why?

00:47:46.990 --> 00:47:49.969
PICKERING: Well, $3 billion is not an irrelevant
factor.

00:47:49.969 --> 00:47:55.799
But, Steve, why don't you go ahead on that,
because I think your point that that seems

00:47:55.799 --> 00:48:01.390
to have escaped us I'm not sure is totally
the case.

00:48:01.390 --> 00:48:05.809
Tell us what you think, and whether there
is anything further to be generated.

00:48:05.809 --> 00:48:10.869
We've sort of looked at the EU connection
through Turkey-Cyprus, but are there other

00:48:10.869 --> 00:48:13.109
things that you see in the EU side?

00:48:13.109 --> 00:48:18.900
COOK: Well, look, just-I mean, my view of
that arc of the last decade with the EU is

00:48:18.900 --> 00:48:27.269
that in late 2004, early 2005, the Turks finally
got an invitation to begin negotiations.

00:48:27.269 --> 00:48:31.599
The theory from the time of the EU was if
we proceed with negotiations the Turks will

00:48:31.599 --> 00:48:36.329
lock in the reforms that they've promised
to do, and will continue to do more reforms.

00:48:36.329 --> 00:48:40.380
But no one actually ever really-in Europe-ever
really expected the Turks to do anything.

00:48:40.380 --> 00:48:44.589
And almost immediately the Europeans decided
that they would put the process really in

00:48:44.589 --> 00:48:45.589
a deep freeze.

00:48:45.589 --> 00:48:50.239
And this had a dynamic effect on the Turks,
who started to see this as not really much

00:48:50.239 --> 00:48:53.319
of a process, but they didn't want to let
the Europeans off the hook.

00:48:53.319 --> 00:48:57.890
The Europeans didn't want to see-be seen as
being anti-Muslim.

00:48:57.890 --> 00:48:59.260
And the Europeans didn't want to let them
off the hook.

00:48:59.260 --> 00:49:07.599
I think the current discussions, in ways the
Turks have turned the tables on the Europeans,

00:49:07.599 --> 00:49:12.130
which is, you know, the Europeans have suddenly
discovered that they really do need the Turks

00:49:12.130 --> 00:49:13.249
on these refugee issues.

00:49:13.249 --> 00:49:17.059
And the Turks have basically taken them to
the cleaners.

00:49:17.059 --> 00:49:19.940
They've let-you know, it's not $3 billion.

00:49:19.940 --> 00:49:21.809
It's closer to $6 billion.

00:49:21.809 --> 00:49:26.589
It's something that they've always wanted,
which is part of the visa-free travel throughout

00:49:26.589 --> 00:49:28.300
Europe in the Schengen Zone.

00:49:28.300 --> 00:49:33.719
And a discussion about how to reenergize EU
negotiations.

00:49:33.719 --> 00:49:40.240
It strikes me that in 2003 and 2004 and part
of 2005, one could imagine the objections

00:49:40.240 --> 00:49:45.529
to Turkey's entry into the European Union
based on kind of technical issues, based on

00:49:45.529 --> 00:49:48.249
political issues and so on and so on being
resolved.

00:49:48.249 --> 00:49:54.930
Given the changes in Turkish domestic politics,
and the profoundly anti-democratic things

00:49:54.930 --> 00:49:59.989
that Erdogan has pursued over the course of
the last five to six years, I can't imagine

00:49:59.989 --> 00:50:01.849
that process moving forward.

00:50:01.849 --> 00:50:06.630
So that what we have here, again, is transactional
relationship within the kind of cover of the

00:50:06.630 --> 00:50:07.630
EU.

00:50:07.630 --> 00:50:11.670
But I don't really expect a lot of progress,
as long as the quality of Turkish politics

00:50:11.670 --> 00:50:12.960
is what it is.

00:50:12.960 --> 00:50:18.829
And going back to something that I think Ambassador
Jeffrey said at the beginning, was-or maybe

00:50:18.829 --> 00:50:22.910
it was you, I'm surrounded by ambassadors,
Ambassador Pickering-that, you know, will

00:50:22.910 --> 00:50:24.619
Erdogan be there?

00:50:24.619 --> 00:50:25.950
How long will he be there?

00:50:25.950 --> 00:50:28.619
He's fully intent at being there at the hundredth
anniversary of the republic.

00:50:28.619 --> 00:50:32.390
And he's fully intent on transforming the
country.

00:50:32.390 --> 00:50:37.619
That, at least in the current iteration of
Erdogan, which is the iteration that we've

00:50:37.619 --> 00:50:44.500
seen since 2007, arguably, doesn't seem compatible
with European entry.

00:50:44.500 --> 00:50:46.180
What do we do?

00:50:46.180 --> 00:50:50.059
I think we continue at the rhetorical level
that we always have, which is Turkey and the

00:50:50.059 --> 00:50:52.219
EU, this is an ongoing process.

00:50:52.219 --> 00:50:56.190
I think if you talk to my friends in New York
who are, you know, bankers and financial engineers,

00:50:56.190 --> 00:51:00.760
and so on and so forth, they regard Turkey
as being part of the European Union.

00:51:00.760 --> 00:51:04.490
And we would be thankful that those things
remain robust, and that that is how Turkey

00:51:04.490 --> 00:51:07.519
is predominately anchored into Western institution.

00:51:07.519 --> 00:51:14.290
PICKERING: The key follow up, is Erdogan at
all now interested in the EU?

00:51:14.290 --> 00:51:22.029
COOK: It strikes me-and maybe my co-panelists
here will have a different view-but it strikes

00:51:22.029 --> 00:51:25.969
me that Erdogan does everything for his own
domestic political purposes.

00:51:25.969 --> 00:51:31.930
And to the extent that it is instrumental
to him in that way, he is.

00:51:31.930 --> 00:51:37.039
He's interested in it now because he-the tables
have turned and the power relationship is

00:51:37.039 --> 00:51:41.119
different, and he's extracted copious amounts
of funds and something that all Turks have

00:51:41.119 --> 00:51:42.160
wanted for a long time.

00:51:42.160 --> 00:51:45.099
PICKERING: And more to come, if he gets-if
he gets right.

00:51:45.099 --> 00:51:46.099
COOK: Yeah.

00:51:46.099 --> 00:51:55.319
PICKERING: And there's perhaps another transactional
piece here that we could incentivize Erdogan

00:51:55.319 --> 00:52:03.799
in terms of trading his serious success with
the EU in domestic politics for slightly less

00:52:03.799 --> 00:52:10.130
irritating domestic posture that could begin
to open the door to something that doesn't

00:52:10.130 --> 00:52:12.220
have a half-life of 25 years anymore.

00:52:12.220 --> 00:52:14.720
COOK: Well, he knows the man better, so.

00:52:14.720 --> 00:52:16.599
PICKERING: Jim, what do you think?

00:52:16.599 --> 00:52:18.450
JEFFREY: It's always possible.

00:52:18.450 --> 00:52:21.770
A, the first rule is, don't hector or lecture
them.

00:52:21.770 --> 00:52:27.219
And our default position, and I speak of me
as an American career diplomat, is to hector

00:52:27.219 --> 00:52:29.049
and-
PICKERING: Hector them.

00:52:29.049 --> 00:52:30.049
(Laughs.)

00:52:30.049 --> 00:52:32.420
JEFFREY: So we have to transcend that.

00:52:32.420 --> 00:52:37.900
And then if you can say: This is how it will
help you.

00:52:37.900 --> 00:52:39.469
We talked about this a little bit before.

00:52:39.469 --> 00:52:44.280
This is a guy on things like the NATO radar
in southeastern Turkey and so on, if you can

00:52:44.280 --> 00:52:47.749
get to a deal with this guy and he says yes,
he will deliver.

00:52:47.749 --> 00:52:48.749
PICKERING: Yeah.

00:52:48.749 --> 00:52:50.460
Have I got a great idea for you, type of thing,
yeah?

00:52:50.460 --> 00:52:51.460
JEFFREY: Yeah.

00:52:51.460 --> 00:52:52.460
Exactly.

00:52:52.460 --> 00:52:55.190
And if you can put it in terms of, look, your
economy is going to tank.

00:52:55.190 --> 00:52:56.230
You're not Russia.

00:52:56.230 --> 00:53:02.660
You don't have the world's largest-or among
the world's largest reserves of oil and gas.

00:53:02.660 --> 00:53:09.319
You have to function in a modern economy and
on the basis of the openness and brains of

00:53:09.319 --> 00:53:10.319
your population.

00:53:10.319 --> 00:53:13.249
And that requires a different domestic approach
than you've got.

00:53:13.249 --> 00:53:15.479
It's not going to get you to a good place.

00:53:15.479 --> 00:53:18.220
That's the kind of thing that he does respond
to.

00:53:18.220 --> 00:53:20.309
I was talking about this earlier.

00:53:20.309 --> 00:53:24.890
That's why he came out today and said: I'm
in favor of secularism in the constitution.

00:53:24.890 --> 00:53:30.020
It's purely cynical, but that's-we got him
where we want him.

00:53:30.020 --> 00:53:31.739
PICKERING: Question here.

00:53:31.739 --> 00:53:33.549
Over here, sir.

00:53:33.549 --> 00:53:36.859
Q: Steve Larrabee, RAND.

00:53:36.859 --> 00:53:40.000
First, question on U.S. leverage.

00:53:40.000 --> 00:53:46.160
It seems to me of all the things you're discussed
up there, or explored, where you might have

00:53:46.160 --> 00:53:53.599
some leverage, the one with the Russians is
the one that's most credible, because Erdogan

00:53:53.599 --> 00:53:59.739
is going to face, and does face, a lot of
questions regarding the Russians.

00:53:59.739 --> 00:54:08.819
And he's not going to like or want to be without
the backing of the United States.

00:54:08.819 --> 00:54:18.030
So it seems to me that's an area where we
could push and try to make certain deals and

00:54:18.030 --> 00:54:19.030
so forth.

00:54:19.030 --> 00:54:25.259
And the other reason why he's not going to
like-want to do it, is not only because Russia

00:54:25.259 --> 00:54:33.509
is a great power with whom they've lost 13
wars historically, but the fact is that not

00:54:33.509 --> 00:54:41.190
many people have looked at-realized this,
but the PYD has opened an office in Moscow.

00:54:41.190 --> 00:54:49.109
This is a real signal to the Kurds that-from
Putin-is very bluntly and directly to them:

00:54:49.109 --> 00:54:55.410
Look, we can make life very difficult for
you, as we did at some times during the Cold

00:54:55.410 --> 00:55:02.270
War, which we haven't done lately, but if
you're going to play hard ball, we're going

00:55:02.270 --> 00:55:03.609
to play harder ball.

00:55:03.609 --> 00:55:13.130
And that, I think, may have even more influence
on his thinking, because he doesn't want to

00:55:13.130 --> 00:55:17.219
see Russians mucking around with the Kurds.

00:55:17.219 --> 00:55:22.170
That really would force a real problem.

00:55:22.170 --> 00:55:26.540
Secondly, the question-
PICKERING: Why don't we do that question,

00:55:26.540 --> 00:55:29.229
and then we'll get around to you again?

00:55:29.229 --> 00:55:37.989
Comments on Russia, Kurdish protection, protecting
the Turks in the case of Russia?

00:55:37.989 --> 00:55:42.359
As I said earlier on, we've already bought
and paid for that in Article 5.

00:55:42.359 --> 00:55:45.599
What do we need to do next?

00:55:45.599 --> 00:55:49.269
JEFFREY: Yeah, I've been trying to restrain
myself from criticizing the administration

00:55:49.269 --> 00:55:52.690
this whole meeting, but now I'm going to have
to come out-(laughter)-of my role as a member

00:55:52.690 --> 00:55:53.690
of the NSC.

00:55:53.690 --> 00:55:54.690
PICKERING: Go ahead.

00:55:54.690 --> 00:55:58.809
JEFFREY: And it's a little bit like we keep
telling the Gulf States: We've got your back,

00:55:58.809 --> 00:56:05.209
with Iran, we'll sell you better missiles
and start integrating your air defense systems.

00:56:05.209 --> 00:56:07.460
And they say this isn't about air defense.

00:56:07.460 --> 00:56:13.309
It's about how you act when suddenly we see,
you know, a Russian taskforce showing up in

00:56:13.309 --> 00:56:15.380
Syria bombing Sunni Arabs.

00:56:15.380 --> 00:56:17.239
What's your response, America?

00:56:17.239 --> 00:56:19.380
Well, we issued a statement.

00:56:19.380 --> 00:56:24.859
Anyway, and so the whole Article 5 thing,
we agree with you.

00:56:24.859 --> 00:56:25.920
You're absolutely right.

00:56:25.920 --> 00:56:27.059
That's the way to go.

00:56:27.059 --> 00:56:32.490
But then you can't do things, and again two
examples in the last year, pulling the Patriots

00:56:32.490 --> 00:56:37.380
out of Turkey after the shoot down, and pulling
the aircraft carrier out of the Middle East

00:56:37.380 --> 00:56:38.680
just before the shoot down.

00:56:38.680 --> 00:56:45.730
Both of them, as far as I can tell, were technical
military reasons that normally the reason

00:56:45.730 --> 00:56:49.999
that you have-one of the reasons, America,
you have the NSC is to tell the military not

00:56:49.999 --> 00:56:55.160
to do what makes most sense from a budget
and mechanical and maintenance standpoint,

00:56:55.160 --> 00:56:56.990
because it will hurt foreign policy.

00:56:56.990 --> 00:57:03.480
So that gets to, if we really believe that
what we're seeing with Russia is generally

00:57:03.480 --> 00:57:09.339
analogous to Russia going into Afghanistan,
Russian going into Ukraine, Russian trying

00:57:09.339 --> 00:57:14.479
to intervene in the Yom Kippur War, and our
responses will be at that kind of very serious

00:57:14.479 --> 00:57:20.680
level, then flowing from that can be specific
nuts and bolts policies and positions and

00:57:20.680 --> 00:57:24.930
maneuvers and sights and deployments that
all fit into that thing.

00:57:24.930 --> 00:57:30.020
If we're ambivalent about this, and I would
argue we are ambivalent about that every at

00:57:30.020 --> 00:57:34.670
least third day, it's kind of, well, maybe
it's a good thing we've got Russia now as

00:57:34.670 --> 00:57:35.670
an ally against ISIS.

00:57:35.670 --> 00:57:38.989
I mean, we're not talking with one voice on
this issue.

00:57:38.989 --> 00:57:43.849
As long as we're not talking with one voice
on the basic, fundamental security problems

00:57:43.849 --> 00:57:48.980
of the region, it's hard to work out an implementing
strategy that will have any legs.

00:57:48.980 --> 00:57:57.390
PICKERING: There's a long history, Jim, if
we go back to Berlin, to Budapest, to Prague,

00:57:57.390 --> 00:57:58.579
on the one hand.

00:57:58.579 --> 00:58:02.559
Secondly, the public appetite for another
deep military engagement in the Middle East,

00:58:02.559 --> 00:58:05.079
to say nothing of the nuclear confrontation.

00:58:05.079 --> 00:58:10.599
Mr. Putin is obviously a legacy and a problem
that has to be dealt with that's not so easy

00:58:10.599 --> 00:58:11.910
to deal with.

00:58:11.910 --> 00:58:17.579
And how far and how much we put ourselves
forward, and how we could shape that as a

00:58:17.579 --> 00:58:22.979
kind of useful to the Turks new gesture, you've,
I think, outlined very clearly what would

00:58:22.979 --> 00:58:23.979
have to be done.

00:58:23.979 --> 00:58:29.299
And I think that outline would probably in
some ways not stand scrutiny in the present

00:58:29.299 --> 00:58:31.089
White House.

00:58:31.089 --> 00:58:34.099
Maybe in a future White House, it might.

00:58:34.099 --> 00:58:40.240
But obviously, moving down small carrots for
the Turks and large consequences with the

00:58:40.240 --> 00:58:44.329
Russians may not be the wisest choice in policy.

00:58:44.329 --> 00:58:45.329
(Laughter.)

00:58:45.329 --> 00:58:46.329
Out there, more questions.

00:58:46.329 --> 00:58:47.329
Right here, sir.

00:58:47.329 --> 00:58:51.859
The microphone is by your right hand.

00:58:51.859 --> 00:58:52.859
Good.

00:58:52.859 --> 00:58:53.859
Q: Yes.

00:58:53.859 --> 00:58:54.859
Thank you.

00:58:54.859 --> 00:58:55.859
I'm the ambassador of Cyprus.

00:58:55.859 --> 00:58:56.859
PICKERING: Nice to see you.

00:58:56.859 --> 00:58:57.859
And thank you.

00:58:57.859 --> 00:58:59.480
Thank you for putting up with our optimism.

00:58:59.480 --> 00:59:00.480
(Laughter.)

00:59:00.480 --> 00:59:06.799
Q: Well, I will try to add, but a little bit
of note of caution as well.

00:59:06.799 --> 00:59:13.430
Well, I would agree that there is considerable
hope for a Cyprus settlement this year.

00:59:13.430 --> 00:59:19.780
The reason is most analysts, if not all analysts,
would agree is that we have two leaders in

00:59:19.780 --> 00:59:22.499
Cyprus that really want this.

00:59:22.499 --> 00:59:27.950
It's the election of Mr. Akinci that has made
the big difference.

00:59:27.950 --> 00:59:33.880
It's also known-well-known that Turkey has
been making very positive noises vis-�-vis

00:59:33.880 --> 00:59:41.900
its attention on Cyprus in the last few months,
since the negotiations have restarted.

00:59:41.900 --> 00:59:49.309
But what is needed now is for Turkey to deliver
on specifics and to deliver soon, by the end

00:59:49.309 --> 00:59:51.130
of the year, perhaps.

00:59:51.130 --> 00:59:58.489
We need some constructive decisions on the
part of Turkey on things that Mr. Akinci and

00:59:58.489 --> 01:00:06.680
the Cypriots themselves cannot decide-things
like the troops and so on, that are 100 percent

01:00:06.680 --> 01:00:08.660
absolute Turkey.

01:00:08.660 --> 01:00:11.049
We worry about three things, basically.

01:00:11.049 --> 01:00:13.989
And like I said, I've only been here a short
time.

01:00:13.989 --> 01:00:20.999
And listening to you today, it reinforces
some of these worries that we have.

01:00:20.999 --> 01:00:27.660
First of all, in transactional terms, is there
enough in a Cyprus deal for the Turks?

01:00:27.660 --> 01:00:31.140
And where would it come from?

01:00:31.140 --> 01:00:32.730
Would it come from Europe?

01:00:32.730 --> 01:00:40.460
Obviously that's problematical, because the
Europeans may not have the intention to accept

01:00:40.460 --> 01:00:44.130
Turkey as a full member, as we know.

01:00:44.130 --> 01:00:48.589
Where could this payback come from?

01:00:48.589 --> 01:00:59.049
Secondly, is Mr. Erdogan reliable or reasonable
enough to be expected to make the right decisions?

01:00:59.049 --> 01:01:08.279
And thirdly, since American influence seems
to be diminishing on Turkey, who will be the

01:01:08.279 --> 01:01:13.589
one that will convince the Turks to do the
right thing?

01:01:13.589 --> 01:01:19.349
And the remnants of American influence on
Turkey, if I may call it that, would the U.S.

01:01:19.349 --> 01:01:25.820
be willing to use them for Cyprus, rather
than one of these other very important interests

01:01:25.820 --> 01:01:26.970
that the U.S. has in the region?

01:01:26.970 --> 01:01:28.650
PICKERING: Well, thank you, your excellency.

01:01:28.650 --> 01:01:33.150
We always make a practice of having pertinent
foreign ambassadors at our NSC meetings to

01:01:33.150 --> 01:01:36.589
make sure we get it right.

01:01:36.589 --> 01:01:37.589
(Laughter.)

01:01:37.589 --> 01:01:42.289
Would any of you like to make a-your points
strike me as reflecting some of the same comments

01:01:42.289 --> 01:01:46.559
that we have made here, but would any of you
like to carry this on a little bit further?

01:01:46.559 --> 01:01:52.630
This is not a Cyprus negotiation policy decisions
process, so I think the real question is how

01:01:52.630 --> 01:01:55.190
it relates to the discussion we're having
of Turkey.

01:01:55.190 --> 01:01:56.190
Jim?

01:01:56.190 --> 01:02:03.829
JEFFREY: Very quickly, yes, I think the U.S.
would use whatever leverage it can with Turkey.

01:02:03.829 --> 01:02:09.069
I think Cyprus is such a big thing, not just
as a domestic issue but in the whole context

01:02:09.069 --> 01:02:13.880
of the eastern Mediterranean today, gas policy
out of Israel and such.

01:02:13.880 --> 01:02:19.430
I think it would be, A, a very wise move to
put a lot of weight into this.

01:02:19.430 --> 01:02:26.300
B, I think that it would be something that
this administration, or almost any administration,

01:02:26.300 --> 01:02:27.300
would do.

01:02:27.300 --> 01:02:30.829
I think Erdogan can be trusted to deliver.

01:02:30.829 --> 01:02:36.799
What Erdogan-if I were sitting up here as
Erdogan, I would say to you: Hey, I delivered

01:02:36.799 --> 01:02:41.219
the vote in Northern Cyprus in 2004.

01:02:41.219 --> 01:02:42.510
You guys didn't.

01:02:42.510 --> 01:02:45.969
I understand you asking me, can you trust
me.

01:02:45.969 --> 01:02:47.559
But I got to ask you the same question.

01:02:47.559 --> 01:02:49.680
So that's how that argument will go.

01:02:49.680 --> 01:02:53.219
PICKERING: Another question, please?

01:02:53.219 --> 01:02:55.880
Yes, right here.

01:02:55.880 --> 01:02:57.650
Got it.

01:02:57.650 --> 01:02:58.650
Thanks.

01:02:58.650 --> 01:03:00.890
Q: My name is Jeremy Young.

01:03:00.890 --> 01:03:04.299
I'm an investigative journalist with Al Jazeera.

01:03:04.299 --> 01:03:09.479
One issue that hasn't come up so far today
is press freedom or, in the case of Turkey,

01:03:09.479 --> 01:03:10.479
lack thereof.

01:03:10.479 --> 01:03:17.201
I looked up this morning, their ranking on
the World Press Freedom Index is 151st, right

01:03:17.201 --> 01:03:21.369
below Tajikistan, which I'm pretty sure is
not a good place to be.

01:03:21.369 --> 01:03:24.839
And so I'm wondering if you can provide us
with a snapshot as to what's happening, how-

01:03:24.839 --> 01:03:27.369
PICKERING: It's a lot better than being above
Tajikistan, I suppose.

01:03:27.369 --> 01:03:30.619
Q: Well, they're one place above the DRC.

01:03:30.619 --> 01:03:34.959
But anyway, could you give us a snapshot as
to what's happening, why that ranking is so

01:03:34.959 --> 01:03:37.930
low, and is the U.S. doing anything about
it?

01:03:37.930 --> 01:03:42.449
Is this-did it not come up today because it's
just not as big of a priority versus all of

01:03:42.449 --> 01:03:44.109
these other issues we're discussing?

01:03:44.109 --> 01:03:45.210
PICKERING: Steve?

01:03:45.210 --> 01:03:46.309
COOK: Sure.

01:03:46.309 --> 01:03:55.219
What has been happening, Jeremy, as you well
know, is that Erdogan and the AKP have pursued

01:03:55.219 --> 01:03:59.999
a strategy of essentially creating a virtual
Ministry of Information.

01:03:59.999 --> 01:04:06.109
They have made it harder and harder and harder
for Turks to get alternative perspectives

01:04:06.109 --> 01:04:09.779
on the news, opinions about Turkish politics.

01:04:09.779 --> 01:04:16.140
And they have used terrorism laws, they have
used political patronage and a variety of

01:04:16.140 --> 01:04:20.959
things essentially to squeeze press freedoms
in Turkey.

01:04:20.959 --> 01:04:26.549
At moments when-this got much worse after
Ambassador Jeffrey was in Turkey, but-

01:04:26.549 --> 01:04:28.240
JEFFREY: After he left Turkey.

01:04:28.240 --> 01:04:29.240
(Laughter.)

01:04:29.240 --> 01:04:30.390
COOK: After you left Turkey, right.

01:04:30.390 --> 01:04:31.609
That's what I meant.

01:04:31.609 --> 01:04:32.839
That's what I meant.

01:04:32.839 --> 01:04:37.579
After you left Turkey, when his successor,
and when then-Secretary Clinton brought these

01:04:37.579 --> 01:04:43.799
issues up, in particular the American ambassador
was essentially told by the Turks to shut

01:04:43.799 --> 01:04:46.759
up and mind their own business.

01:04:46.759 --> 01:04:48.700
It is a huge problem.

01:04:48.700 --> 01:04:53.250
And it is-when I talk about this sort of U-turn,
this authoritarian turn in Turkish politics,

01:04:53.250 --> 01:04:55.799
this is one of the things that I'm thinking
of.

01:04:55.799 --> 01:05:03.569
When we are thinking about-kind of broadly
about U.S. strategic interests, and how we

01:05:03.569 --> 01:05:08.590
are prioritizing the fight against the Islamic
State, the mess that is Iraq, and the kind

01:05:08.590 --> 01:05:13.979
of geostrategic issues of the region, whether
it's the eastern Mediterranean or the Middle

01:05:13.979 --> 01:05:20.190
East, the question that we confront is: Do
we address these issues of Turkish domestic

01:05:20.190 --> 01:05:24.539
politics at the risk of angering the Turks
and undermining some of the things that we

01:05:24.539 --> 01:05:29.140
want to do with them around the region that
will be good for us, or do we want to take

01:05:29.140 --> 01:05:31.839
a full-throated stand on that?

01:05:31.839 --> 01:05:36.819
Up until this point, it strikes me that it's
clear that the administration has chosen the

01:05:36.819 --> 01:05:42.210
latter rather than the former, but I think
as we pointed out, these are really-these

01:05:42.210 --> 01:05:48.359
are difficult issues, given Turkey's geography
and that it touches almost every important

01:05:48.359 --> 01:05:52.709
critical-whatever Washington talking point
it is-issue that the United States confronts

01:05:52.709 --> 01:05:56.509
in the Middle East and beyond-Caucasus, Russia,
et cetera.

01:05:56.509 --> 01:05:59.749
PICKERING: How much Turkish language broadcasting
do we do?

01:05:59.749 --> 01:06:03.180
Jim, do you have any idea?

01:06:03.180 --> 01:06:07.329
JEFFREY: We did do some, but not very much.

01:06:07.329 --> 01:06:12.719
And that's, of course, the fallacy of what
Erdogan does, because the Turks are really

01:06:12.719 --> 01:06:16.650
very good-just like every other-denizen of
any totalitarian, authoritarian, or repressive

01:06:16.650 --> 01:06:22.609
government, state in the world-at finding
out what's going on in the world.

01:06:22.609 --> 01:06:26.329
And they're fully-we do raise it.

01:06:26.329 --> 01:06:30.039
We have to raise it, simply to be honest with
ourselves.

01:06:30.039 --> 01:06:33.869
But we have to raise it with the understanding
that it ain't going to make any difference

01:06:33.869 --> 01:06:34.869
to Erdogan.

01:06:34.869 --> 01:06:41.239
PICKERING: My view is that issues like that,
traditionally we do better when we have better

01:06:41.239 --> 01:06:48.430
relations going on or we have a dependency
relationship in which for them the relationship

01:06:48.430 --> 01:06:53.640
with the United States is more important than
it is oppressing the press or dealing badly

01:06:53.640 --> 01:07:00.039
with civil rights, and that that's the kind
of relationship that this group is now seeking

01:07:00.039 --> 01:07:03.339
to try to move us toward.

01:07:03.339 --> 01:07:08.050
And raising the question consistently in a
useful way is something that I don't think

01:07:08.050 --> 01:07:10.319
we ever put off the agenda.

01:07:10.319 --> 01:07:15.960
But having hopes by continuing to do the same
thing we're going to get a different result,

01:07:15.960 --> 01:07:20.559
without changing the backdrop, if I could
put it that, or the relationship, is a harder

01:07:20.559 --> 01:07:24.499
question to solve.

01:07:24.499 --> 01:07:26.249
Question?

01:07:26.249 --> 01:07:27.650
We've exhausted you.

01:07:27.650 --> 01:07:28.650
OK, right here.

01:07:28.650 --> 01:07:29.650
Q: Patrick Theros.

01:07:29.650 --> 01:07:32.599
If anybody out there knows me, please don't
tell.

01:07:32.599 --> 01:07:34.579
PICKERING: Patrick, great to see you.

01:07:34.579 --> 01:07:35.579
COOK: Another ambassador.

01:07:35.579 --> 01:07:36.579
PICKERING: Yeah.

01:07:36.579 --> 01:07:37.579
Q: Yeah.

01:07:37.579 --> 01:07:38.579
A question.

01:07:38.579 --> 01:07:39.579
Erdogan is a truly brilliant negotiator.

01:07:39.579 --> 01:07:42.499
We just watched him take the Europeans to
the cleaners.

01:07:42.499 --> 01:07:45.199
He's done this on several occasions.

01:07:45.199 --> 01:07:47.809
And-
PICKERING: That's from a Greek American, so

01:07:47.809 --> 01:07:48.809
speak up.

01:07:48.809 --> 01:07:53.319
Q: I stand in awe of Mr. Erdogan, actually,
in many ways.

01:07:53.319 --> 01:07:59.549
The problem is that he is a brilliant negotiator,
knows how to push.

01:07:59.549 --> 01:08:02.279
And everybody who's been negotiating with
him never pushes back.

01:08:02.279 --> 01:08:07.910
There is a long-term downside to Mr. Erdogan's
policy which could mean some very serious

01:08:07.910 --> 01:08:09.440
trouble for the state of Turkey.

01:08:09.440 --> 01:08:14.410
And it appears that we're all dancing around,
not telling him that left to his own devices

01:08:14.410 --> 01:08:16.810
he could very much wreck the Turkish state.

01:08:16.810 --> 01:08:21.180
Shouldn't there be-shouldn't there be sticks
on this one as well as carrots?

01:08:21.180 --> 01:08:22.180
PICKERING: Jim, would you have a comment?

01:08:22.180 --> 01:08:23.180
You've deal with him?

01:08:23.180 --> 01:08:25.710
JEFFREY: Yeah, I kind of did this in passing
at the beginning, when I said we may need

01:08:25.710 --> 01:08:31.240
to get back to first of yours because from
a pure realpolitik standpoint, the platform

01:08:31.240 --> 01:08:37.830
that we're expecting to do all these things
and-(inaudible)-is a fragile platform because

01:08:37.830 --> 01:08:38.950
of what he's doing.

01:08:38.950 --> 01:08:40.210
So I think that's a message.

01:08:40.210 --> 01:08:45.060
But again, I'll get to Tom's point which was,
as everything Tom says, brilliant.

01:08:45.060 --> 01:08:47.660
We have to have a better backdrop of relations.

01:08:47.660 --> 01:08:54.540
The more they think we really have their back
on key security things, the more they're willing

01:08:54.540 --> 01:09:03.800
to listen privately and quietly to us talking
to them about-or not them, him-domestic political

01:09:03.800 --> 01:09:04.800
issues.

01:09:04.800 --> 01:09:05.800
It may not work in the end.

01:09:05.800 --> 01:09:07.300
And he may just drive it off the cliff.

01:09:07.300 --> 01:09:11.000
But we'll have-we would have a fighting chance
with this guy.

01:09:11.000 --> 01:09:16.320
And in terms of pushing back against him,
the negotiations-or a lot of them that I've

01:09:16.320 --> 01:09:24.170
been in-if you come up with an alternative
and tell him, look, you know, oh great leader,

01:09:24.170 --> 01:09:28.390
we want to do this with you but if you don't
want to do this, we understand, we're going

01:09:28.390 --> 01:09:30.380
to go off and do it with somebody else.

01:09:30.380 --> 01:09:31.840
That kind of gets his attention.

01:09:31.840 --> 01:09:37.040
I almost got the feeling he likes it when
that happens, because it's treating him seriously.

01:09:37.040 --> 01:09:42.030
So he's a guy that you can, and we do, push
back against from time to time, and it does

01:09:42.030 --> 01:09:43.030
work.

01:09:43.030 --> 01:09:46.550
PICKERING: We're now at the period where we're
going to do a brief windup.

01:09:46.550 --> 01:09:50.990
We talked about a pretty broad menu, maybe
even a smorgasbord.

01:09:50.990 --> 01:09:54.100
I'd like to hear your windup comments.

01:09:54.100 --> 01:10:01.330
But give us a sense of which of these potentially
useful but apparently feckless items we've

01:10:01.330 --> 01:10:07.010
discussed here you would put at the top of
the priority to try out, just to give us a

01:10:07.010 --> 01:10:08.840
little sense of how you see the priorities.

01:10:08.840 --> 01:10:12.040
Maybe, Melissa, since we haven't called on
you, we could ask you to lead off.

01:10:12.040 --> 01:10:13.040
DALTON: Sure.

01:10:13.040 --> 01:10:18.310
I think, you know, a common theme, and to
bucket some of the options that have been

01:10:18.310 --> 01:10:23.220
discussed, is going back to this idea of thinking
about Turkey in the broader milieu of the

01:10:23.220 --> 01:10:24.220
security environment.

01:10:24.220 --> 01:10:25.860
Like, it's not just about the Middle East.

01:10:25.860 --> 01:10:28.920
It also makes sense to parts of Europe.

01:10:28.920 --> 01:10:33.550
So whether that's thinking about Turkey and
its role in deterring Russian aggression,

01:10:33.550 --> 01:10:39.860
whether it's repairing relationship with Israel,
fusing intelligence and operations on the

01:10:39.860 --> 01:10:45.460
Iraqi side of the board, thinking of Turkey
as it sits in its geostrategic position as

01:10:45.460 --> 01:10:52.600
a network-a node in the network I think is
really important going forward.

01:10:52.600 --> 01:11:00.930
And I would add to the discussion on the Syrian-Kurd
dynamic, that perhaps it's worth a constructive

01:11:00.930 --> 01:11:07.410
and creative conversation with the Turks that
their evolution in how they have thought about

01:11:07.410 --> 01:11:14.500
the Iraqi Kurds is quite interesting, where
they now think of them as somewhat of a partner,

01:11:14.500 --> 01:11:18.550
somebody that they can work from an economic
perspective, from an overall cooperative perspective.

01:11:18.550 --> 01:11:25.720
Is there a future in which the Turks can imagine
a Syrian Kurdish entity that resembles the

01:11:25.720 --> 01:11:26.720
KRG?

01:11:26.720 --> 01:11:33.051
It may not be the YPD or YPG, but, you know,
is that some sort of common vision that we

01:11:33.051 --> 01:11:34.490
could work towards?

01:11:34.490 --> 01:11:35.970
So I'd just leave that there.

01:11:35.970 --> 01:11:36.970
PICKERING: Thanks, Melissa.

01:11:36.970 --> 01:11:41.900
And I think that is sort of the natural evolution
from working with the Iraqi Kurds.

01:11:41.900 --> 01:11:46.610
You know, there are examples and ways that
one could look at presumably greater autonomy,

01:11:46.610 --> 01:11:51.730
another good reason for not pushing the Iraqi
Kurd process over the edge, if I could put

01:11:51.730 --> 01:11:53.590
it that way, which is a greater catastrophe.

01:11:53.590 --> 01:11:58.830
Jim, do you want to-I'm giving Steve the first
and last comments.

01:11:58.830 --> 01:12:00.080
Would you rather have the last comments?

01:12:00.080 --> 01:12:01.080
It's your choice.

01:12:01.080 --> 01:12:05.560
JEFFREY: No, because I'm dying to make my
little witty comment.

01:12:05.560 --> 01:12:06.560
(Laughter.)

01:12:06.560 --> 01:12:09.970
Rather than smorgasbord, why don't we talk
about the meselehr (ph) of issues.

01:12:09.970 --> 01:12:10.970
(Laughter.)

01:12:10.970 --> 01:12:11.970
COOK: Well played.

01:12:11.970 --> 01:12:12.970
(Laughter.)

01:12:12.970 --> 01:12:20.540
JEFFREY: I would second the Kurdish one, and
just add a rational approach to Syria that

01:12:20.540 --> 01:12:26.040
will clip the wings of some of Erdogan's more
ambitious and wilder ideas, but get us with

01:12:26.040 --> 01:12:31.460
a little bit more skin in the game without,
as you said, provoking a serious confrontation

01:12:31.460 --> 01:12:32.460
with the Russians.

01:12:32.460 --> 01:12:33.980
I think there's room to do that.

01:12:33.980 --> 01:12:43.360
You combine that with the Kurdish issue, and
a little bit more muscle on the bones of the

01:12:43.360 --> 01:12:48.420
NATO commitments to these guys, and that would
give you the backdrop that would allow you

01:12:48.420 --> 01:12:54.380
to talk about a whole lot of things, from
Cyprus to Israel to press freedom, frankly.

01:12:54.380 --> 01:12:55.490
PICKERING: Thank you, Jim.

01:12:55.490 --> 01:12:56.490
Steve.

01:12:56.490 --> 01:13:01.680
COOK: Let me just, on the record, suggesting
that I'm deeply pessimistic that we will be

01:13:01.680 --> 01:13:04.810
able to move the Turks, at least through the
duration of this administration.

01:13:04.810 --> 01:13:09.550
It is going to require us to take a different
approach to the Russians, a different approach

01:13:09.550 --> 01:13:13.160
to Syria, and a different approach to the
Kurds.

01:13:13.160 --> 01:13:17.140
But one thing that we could be doing and we
should be doing, and I think that-and Jim

01:13:17.140 --> 01:13:21.840
kind of hinted at this-and the way in which
you have to talk to Erdogan, and that is to

01:13:21.840 --> 01:13:26.270
play to his vanity about who he is and where
he's taking Turkey and the transformation

01:13:26.270 --> 01:13:31.200
of Turkey, and that some of the things that
he has done, both domestically and abroad,

01:13:31.200 --> 01:13:33.360
can undermine this project.

01:13:33.360 --> 01:13:38.110
That his-there are hints, there are glimmers
that he understands that Turkey's strategic

01:13:38.110 --> 01:13:44.290
position in the region has diminished significantly
since 2011 to 2012.

01:13:44.290 --> 01:13:49.290
Part of that is a function of his domestic
politics, and that he should think about-he

01:13:49.290 --> 01:13:54.520
knows where he wants to go, but that there
are all of these things around him that can

01:13:54.520 --> 01:13:57.540
undermine that, and that we can help him achieve
that.

01:13:57.540 --> 01:14:03.100
I think, you know, too often there are cries,
you know, including from myself, that we should,

01:14:03.100 --> 01:14:05.320
you know, kind of put the squeeze on Erdogan.

01:14:05.320 --> 01:14:07.810
And certainly the press freedom, those kinds
of things.

01:14:07.810 --> 01:14:12.310
But at the same time, if we're never going
to get-if we feel like we have to say those

01:14:12.310 --> 01:14:15.250
things, but we're never going to get there,
we might as well try another track.

01:14:15.250 --> 01:14:18.430
And lord knows he thinks well of himself.

01:14:18.430 --> 01:14:22.670
So we may want to use that on some of these
issues in our kind of broader discussion.

01:14:22.670 --> 01:14:25.970
PICKERING: Just a brief final comment from
me.

01:14:25.970 --> 01:14:31.950
I think successful policy requires a pretty
clear sense of the objective.

01:14:31.950 --> 01:14:36.190
I think we've tried to give you that today
in the various discussions we've had of the

01:14:36.190 --> 01:14:39.470
policy focus and where it tends to go.

01:14:39.470 --> 01:14:44.910
We've also tried to look pretty broadly at
places where in the transactional aspect of

01:14:44.910 --> 01:14:50.320
this, which is more than just a quid pro quo,
it's the building of confidence and the building

01:14:50.320 --> 01:14:52.980
of a relationship that it's very important.

01:14:52.980 --> 01:15:00.050
I think any meeting of the NSC like this has
to evolve something that's more of a decision

01:15:00.050 --> 01:15:03.330
to go for one of these particular pieces.

01:15:03.330 --> 01:15:10.110
You have to look even in this pretty broad
stovepipe at how the synergies might work,

01:15:10.110 --> 01:15:15.320
how the syncopation, the timing, and the development
of the efforts might be.

01:15:15.320 --> 01:15:19.880
What you need to look at is clearly-and we
haven't had the time to do that-as much of

01:15:19.880 --> 01:15:24.000
the downsides as the upsides, although you've
all tried to help and I think been voices

01:15:24.000 --> 01:15:28.010
of clarity, concern, and understanding of
what this is.

01:15:28.010 --> 01:15:35.100
But this is obviously not even a reasonable
facsimile of a real meeting, but certainly

01:15:35.100 --> 01:15:40.310
you can count on yourselves of having had
an ear and eyesight at the beginning of the

01:15:40.310 --> 01:15:43.500
creation of our new Turkish policy here at
CFR.

01:15:43.500 --> 01:15:44.500
(Laughter.)

01:15:44.500 --> 01:15:50.430
And for that, we're very grateful for your
support of CFR, your attendance today, and

01:15:50.430 --> 01:15:55.770
for your willingness, obviously, to give us
your thoughts as this process proceeded.

01:15:55.770 --> 01:15:57.110
Thank you very much for coming.

01:15:57.110 --> 01:16:03.380
But it remains for me also to thank on your
behalf, Steve, and Melissa, and Jim, for their

01:16:03.380 --> 01:16:09.720
offerings, which I think were rich, very helpful,
and very constructive, but also very objective.

01:16:09.720 --> 01:16:14.570
And so thank you for keeping that balance,
which sometimes is very hard to get.

01:16:14.570 --> 01:16:15.570
So the meeting is adjourned.

01:16:15.570 --> 01:16:16.570
(Applause.)

01:16:16.570 --> 01:16:16.571
(END)

